var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Julia-API","page":"Julia API","text":"The names listed here are public. FFTL exports the names FFTW.jl exports: the DFT and DCT functions with their plan_ and in-place forms, and AbstractFFTs.jl's helpers (fftshift, fftfreq, …). All other names are used with the prefix, FFTL.r2r, FFTL.REDFT10 and so on. The exported functions are FFTL's own; the generic functions of AbstractFFTs.jl reach them through the methods described on AbstractFFTs.","title":"Julia API"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Complex-transforms","page":"Julia API","text":"plan_bfft and plan_ifft are documented with plan_fft; fft!, bfft!, ifft!, plan_bfft! and plan_ifft! with plan_fft!.","title":"Complex transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Real-transforms","page":"Julia API","text":"plan_brfft and plan_irfft are documented with plan_rfft.","title":"Real transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Real-to-real-transforms","page":"Julia API","text":"idct is documented with dct; plan_idct, plan_dct!, plan_idct!, dct! and idct! with plan_dct; r2r with plan_r2r.\n\nThe kinds, as constants: REDFT00 (DCT-I), REDFT10 (DCT-II), REDFT01 (DCT-III), REDFT11 (DCT-IV), RODFT00 (DST-I), RODFT10 (DST-II), RODFT01 (DST-III), RODFT11 (DST-IV), DHT (Hartley), R2HC and HC2R (halfcomplex). Their formulas are on Cosine, sine and Hartley transforms.","title":"Real-to-real transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Passes","page":"Julia API","text":"Batched one-dimensional transforms of strided arrays, the operations NFFT.jl builds on.","title":"Passes"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Run-time-plans","page":"Julia API","text":"Plans whose radix sequences and sizes are data, for programs compiled with juliac --trim; see Compiling ahead of time.\n\nexecute_r2c!, execute_c2r! and execute_r2r! are documented with the plans they apply.","title":"Run-time plans"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#GPU","page":"Julia API","text":"Available after using KernelAbstractions and a GPU package.\n\nplan_ka_brfft and plan_ka_r2r are documented with plan_ka_rfft.","title":"GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Cache-and-configuration","page":"Julia API","text":"","title":"Cache and configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Plan-types","page":"Julia API","text":"Each plan is an AbstractFFTs.Plan. The types are listed for reference; their fields are not part of the interface.\n\ntype returned by\nFFTLPlan plan_fft, plan_bfft, plan_ifft (wrapped in ScaledPlan)\nRFFTLPlan plan_rfft\nBRFFTLPlan plan_brfft, plan_irfft (wrapped in ScaledPlan)\nR2RPlan plan_r2r\nOrthoDCTPlan plan_dct, plan_idct\nInplacePlan plan_fft!, plan_bfft!, plan_ifft!, plan_dct!, plan_idct!\nPassPlan plan_pass\nR2RPassPlan plan_r2r_pass\nGPlan, GRPlan, GR2RPlan the same functions for BigFloat and other number types\n\nThe plans of the generic and the GPU paths follow the same protocol; see AbstractFFTs.","title":"Plan types"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.fft","page":"Julia API","text":"fft(x, region = 1:ndims(x))\n\nForward DFT of x along region (all dimensions by default), unnormalised. Equal to plan_fft(x, region) * x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.fft"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.bfft","page":"Julia API","text":"bfft(x, region = 1:ndims(x))\n\nBackward DFT along region, unnormalised: applying bfft to fft(x) multiplies x by the number of transformed elements.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.bfft"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.ifft","page":"Julia API","text":"ifft(x, region = 1:ndims(x))\n\nNormalised inverse DFT along region: ifft(fft(x)) ≈ x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.ifft"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.plan_fft","page":"Julia API","text":"plan_fft(x::AbstractArray, region = 1:ndims(x); threads, num_threads, flags, timelimit)\nplan_bfft(x, region = 1:ndims(x); …), plan_ifft(x, region = 1:ndims(x); …)\n\nPlans for the forward, unnormalised backward and normalised inverse DFT along the dimensions region of arrays like x. Float32 and Float64 (real or complex) use the SIMD kernels, and transforms large enough to profit run on up to threads threads (default: the value of FFTL.set_num_threads, else Threads.nthreads(); num_threads is a synonym). flags and timelimit are accepted for FFTW.jl compatibility and have no effect: FFTL does not measure while planning. Other floating-point types (BigFloat, Double64, Float128, …) are transformed in their own precision by a generic, single-threaded path; Float16, integers and other numbers are converted to ComplexF64.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.plan_fft"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.plan_fft!","page":"Julia API","text":"plan_fft!(x, region = 1:ndims(x); …), plan_bfft!, plan_ifft!\nfft!(x, region = 1:ndims(x)), bfft!, ifft!\n\nIn-place forms: the plans overwrite the array they are applied to with p * x, and fft!, bfft! and ifft! overwrite x. Keywords as for plan_fft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.plan_fft!"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.InplacePlan","page":"Julia API","text":"InplacePlan(p)\n\nA plan that applies p in place: q * x overwrites x and returns it, as FFTW.jl's in-place plans do. Returned by plan_fft!, plan_bfft!, plan_ifft!, plan_dct! and plan_idct!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.InplacePlan"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.rfft","page":"Julia API","text":"rfft(x, region = 1:ndims(x))\n\nForward DFT of real x, returning the half spectrum along the first dimension of region (size(x, d) ÷ 2 + 1 values there).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.rfft"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.brfft","page":"Julia API","text":"brfft(X, d, region = 1:ndims(X))\n\nUnnormalised inverse of the half spectrum X to a real array of length d along the first dimension of region.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.brfft"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.irfft","page":"Julia API","text":"irfft(X, d, region = 1:ndims(X))\n\nNormalised inverse of the half spectrum X to a real array of length d along the first dimension of region: irfft(rfft(x), length(x)) ≈ x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.irfft"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.plan_rfft","page":"Julia API","text":"plan_rfft(x::AbstractArray{<:Real}, region = 1:ndims(x); threads, num_threads, flags, timelimit)\nplan_brfft(X::AbstractArray{<:Complex}, d, region = 1:ndims(X); …)\nplan_irfft(X::AbstractArray{<:Complex}, d, region = 1:ndims(X); …)\n\nReal-input forward DFT (half spectrum along the first dimension of region), its unnormalised inverse to real arrays with length d along that dimension, and the normalised inverse. Keywords as for plan_fft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.plan_rfft"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.dct","page":"Julia API","text":"dct(x, region = 1:ndims(x)), idct(x, region = 1:ndims(x))\n\nOrthonormal DCT-II and its inverse (DCT-III) along region.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.dct"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.plan_dct","page":"Julia API","text":"plan_dct(x, region = 1:ndims(x); …), plan_idct(x, region = 1:ndims(x); …)\nplan_dct!(x, region = 1:ndims(x); …), plan_idct!(x, region = 1:ndims(x); …)\ndct!(x, region = 1:ndims(x)), idct!(x, region = 1:ndims(x))\n\nPlans of the orthonormal DCT-II and its inverse (the scaling of dct), and their in-place forms. Keywords as for plan_fft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.plan_dct"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.plan_r2r","page":"Julia API","text":"plan_r2r(x::AbstractArray{<:Real}, kinds, region = 1:ndims(x); threads, num_threads, flags, timelimit)\nr2r(x, kinds, region = 1:ndims(x))\n\nUnnormalised real-to-real transforms; kinds is one R2RKind (REDFT00 … RODFT11, DHT, R2HC, HC2R) for all dimensions of region or one per dimension. Keywords as for plan_fft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.plan_r2r"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.R2RKind","page":"Julia API","text":"R2RKind\n\nThe real-to-real kinds: REDFT00 … REDFT11 (DCT-I … DCT-IV), RODFT00 … RODFT11 (DST-I … DST-IV), DHT (Hartley), R2HC and HC2R (halfcomplex). The formulas are in the documentation (Cosine, sine and Hartley transforms).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.R2RKind"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.plan_pass","page":"Julia API","text":"plan_pass(v::StridedArray{<:Complex}, d; sign = -1, threads = 1, nonzero = nothing, needed = nothing)\n\nPlan for the batched 1D DFT along dimension d of arrays with the size and strides of v (sign = -1 forward, +1 unnormalised backward). Apply with mul!(w, p, v) (w with the same size and strides, w === v in place) or apply!(p, v). With threads > 1 the lines are split among threads, and a single long line (d = 1) uses the four-step algorithm; callers that thread over many arrays themselves keep the default 1.\n\nnonzero (ranges of indices along d, e.g. (1:a, b:n)) declares that each line is zero outside these ranges; needed that only these outputs of each line are used (the others may hold anything afterwards). The first resp. last stage then skips the zero inputs resp. the unneeded outputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.plan_pass"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.plan_r2r_pass","page":"Julia API","text":"plan_r2r_pass(v::StridedArray{<:Real}, d, kind; threads = 1)\n\nPlan for the real-to-real transform kind (REDFT00 … RODFT11, DHT, R2HC, HC2R; unnormalised) along dimension d of all lines of arrays with the size and strides of v. Apply in place with apply!(p, v) or into an array of the same layout with mul!(w, p, v).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.plan_r2r_pass"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.apply!","page":"Julia API","text":"apply!(p::PassPlan, v)\n\nThe transform of p applied to v in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.apply!"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.RPlan","page":"Julia API","text":"RPlan{T}(sz, region, σ; threads = Threads.nthreads())\n\nDFT plan with run-time kernels for complex arrays of size sz (any number of dimensions) along the dimensions region, sign σ (-1 forward, +1 unnormalised backward). Applied like any FFTL plan (p * x, mul!(y, p, x), inv(p)); execute!(p, py, px) takes pointers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.RPlan"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.execute!","page":"Julia API","text":"execute!(p::RPlan{T}, py::Ptr{Complex{T}}, px::Ptr{Complex{T}})\n\nApply p to the array at px (column major, size p.sz) and write the result to py; py may equal px. The caller keeps both arrays alive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.execute!"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.RRFFTPlan","page":"Julia API","text":"RRFFTPlan{T}(sz, region, σ; threads = Threads.nthreads())\n\nr2c (σ = -1) or unnormalised c2r (σ = +1) plan for real arrays of size sz along region: real along the first dimension of the region (half spectrum of n ÷ 2 + 1 values there), complex along the others. Applied with execute_r2c!(p, py, px) or execute_c2r!(p, py, px) (pointers; out of place, c2r leaves its input unchanged). In place (py and px the same address) the real array is padded to 2(n ÷ 2 + 1) values along that dimension, so that it fills the memory of the half spectrum (FFTW's in-place layout); the padding is ignored by r2c and undefined after c2r.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.RRFFTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.RR2RPlan","page":"Julia API","text":"RR2RPlan{T}(sz, kinds, region; threads = Threads.nthreads())\n\nReal-to-real plan with run-time kernels for arrays of size sz, one R2RKind per dimension of region (unnormalised, as plan_r2r). Applied with execute_r2r!(p, py, px); py may equal px.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.RR2RPlan"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.plan_ka","page":"Julia API","text":"plan_ka(backend, T, dims, σ; region = 1:length(dims), gmax = 64, radices = nothing)\n\nDFT plan (σ = -1 forward, +1 unnormalised backward) for arrays of size dims with element type Complex{T} on a KernelAbstractions backend (Metal, CUDA, ROCm, oneAPI, CPU), along the dimensions region. Requires using KernelAbstractions; with a GPU package loaded, FFTL.plan_fft(x) etc. return these plans for GPU arrays. Apply with p * x or mul!(y, p, x) (y !== x); kernels are queued on the backend without synchronization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.plan_ka"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.plan_ka_rfft","page":"Julia API","text":"plan_ka_rfft(backend, T, dims; region = 1:length(dims))\nplan_ka_brfft(backend, T, dims; region = 1:length(dims))\nplan_ka_r2r(backend, T, dims, kinds; region = 1:length(dims))\n\nReal-input forward DFT (half spectrum along region[1]), its unnormalised inverse (dims is the size of the real array in both) and real-to-real transforms (one R2RKind per dimension) on a KernelAbstractions backend. Requires using KernelAbstractions; with a GPU package loaded, FFTL.plan_rfft, plan_brfft, plan_irfft and plan_r2r return these plans for GPU arrays.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.plan_ka_rfft"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.set_num_threads","page":"Julia API","text":"FFTL.set_num_threads(n)\n\nDefault number of threads for the plans made afterwards (threads keyword not given), as FFTW.set_num_threads. Without a call, plans use Threads.nthreads().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.set_num_threads"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.ESTIMATE","page":"Julia API","text":"FFTL.ESTIMATE, FFTL.MEASURE, FFTL.PATIENT, FFTL.EXHAUSTIVE\n\nValues for the flags keyword of the plan functions, accepted for FFTW.jl compatibility. They have no effect: an FFTL plan does not depend on measurements made while planning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.ESTIMATE"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.clear_cache!","page":"Julia API","text":"clear_cache!()\n\nDrop the twiddle and filter tables that FFTL keeps for reuse by later plans.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.clear_cache!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.cpu_caches","page":"Julia API","text":"FFTL.cpu_caches()\n\nCache sizes the CPU plans are sized from: L1 data and L2 per core, last-level cache per core cluster, in bytes, and where they come from (:queried, :default, :user).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.cpu_caches"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.set_cpu_caches!","page":"Julia API","text":"FFTL.set_cpu_caches!(; l1, l2, llc)\n\nOverride the cache sizes (bytes) that plans are sized from, e.g. when the query at load time fails or a machine reports misleading values. Affects plans made afterwards. FFTL.set_cpu_caches!() restores the queried values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.set_cpu_caches!"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/api/#FFTL.PRECOMPILE_WORKLOAD","page":"Julia API","text":"FFTL.PRECOMPILE_WORKLOAD\n\nThe precompile workload selected when FFTL was built: \"lean\" (default), \"broad\" or \"none\". It comes from the precompile_workload preference:\n\nusing Preferences, FFTL\nPreferences.set_preferences!(FFTL, \"precompile_workload\" => \"broad\"; force = true)\n\nThe next load recompiles FFTL with the selected workload.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FFTL.PRECOMPILE_WORKLOAD"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/plans/#Plans-and-reuse","page":"Plans and reuse","text":"fft(x) makes a plan, applies it and throws the plan away. That is the right call for a one-off transform. In a loop, make the plan once: everything that depends only on the size and the dimensions is computed in the plan, and applying it costs only the arithmetic.\n\nusing FFTL, LinearAlgebra\n\nx = randn(ComplexF64, 512, 512)\np = plan_fft(x)             # forward plan for arrays like x\ny = similar(x)\nmul!(y, p, x)               # out of place, into a preallocated y\ny ≈ fft(x)\ntrue","title":"Plans and reuse"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/plans/#In-place","page":"Plans and reuse","text":"A plan can transform an array into itself, as mul!(x, p, x). The plan's buffers are used internally, so this does not allocate the output. p * x allocates a new array, and mul!(y, p, x) writes into a preallocated y. The in-place plans of plan_fft!, plan_bfft! and plan_ifft! overwrite their argument with p * x, and fft!(x) transforms x itself, as in FFTW.jl.\n\nx2 = copy(x)\nmul!(x2, p, x2)                  # in place\nx2 ≈ y\ntrue","title":"In place"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/plans/#Inverses","page":"Plans and reuse","text":"plan_ifft and plan_bfft are the normalised and the unnormalised backward transform. An existing forward plan has its inverse as inv(p), and p \\ y applies it:\n\npinv = inv(p)\npinv * y ≈ x\n\np \\ y ≈ x\ntrue","title":"Inverses"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/plans/#One-plan-per-task","page":"Plans and reuse","text":"A plan owns its work buffers. Two tasks applying the same plan at the same time would write into the same buffers, so a plan must not be shared between tasks. Plans of the same size share their twiddle tables and kernels, so making one more plan is cheap. The pattern for a threaded loop is one plan per task, each task working through its own chunk of the items:\n\nchunks = Iterators.partition(eachindex(xs), cld(length(xs), Threads.nthreads()))\n@sync for c in chunks\n  Threads.@spawn begin\n    p = plan_fft(xs[first(c)]; threads = 1)   # this task's plan\n    for i in c\n      mul!(ys[i], p, xs[i])\n    end\n  end\nend\n\nIndexing a vector of plans with Threads.threadid() is not safe: a task can move to another thread while it runs, and two tasks can then use the same plan.","title":"One plan per task"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/plans/#Threads-inside-a-plan","page":"Plans and reuse","text":"Plans take a threads keyword, which defaults to Threads.nthreads(). Code that is already threaded over its own work should pass threads = 1, see Threads.\n\np1 = plan_fft(x; threads = 1)\np1 * x ≈ fft(x)\ntrue","title":"Threads inside a plan"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/plans/#When-a-plan-is-expensive","page":"Plans and reuse","text":"A plan for a new radix sequence compiles its kernels, which takes milliseconds for the sizes in the precompile workload and up to a few seconds for others. After that, plans of the same size are a lookup in a cache, and transforms never compile. Kernels are kept in a cache of 128 entries that drops the least recently used entry when it is full (FFTL.clear_cache!() empties it). To remove the compilation cost from the first call entirely, see Precompilation.","title":"When a plan is expensive"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/real/#Real-data","page":"Real data","text":"Real input has a spectrum with Hermitian symmetry: X_N-k = overlineX_k. Half of it is redundant, and the real transforms return only the other half. That halves the work and the memory.\n\nusing FFTL, Random\nRandom.seed!(5)\n\nx = randn(1000, 300)\nX = rfft(x)\nsize(X)\n(501, 300)\n\nThe first transformed dimension is halved: 1000  2 + 1 = 501 values for frequencies 0  500. The other dimensions keep their length. The complex array has the same type as a complex transform's output.","title":"Real data"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/real/#Going-back","page":"Real data","text":"The inverse needs the length of the real array, because an odd and an even length can give the same half spectrum. irfft takes it explicitly:\n\nirfft(X, 1000) ≈ x\ntrue\n\ny = randn(999)\nY = rfft(y)\nsize(Y), irfft(Y, 999) ≈ y\n((500,), true)\n\nbrfft is the unnormalised inverse, the real counterpart of bfft. It differs from irfft by the product of the transformed lengths, the number of elements in the region; for one dimension, the length of the real array:\n\ny1 = randn(999)\nY1 = rfft(y1)\nbrfft(Y1, 999) ≈ 999 .* irfft(Y1, 999)\ntrue","title":"Going back"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/real/#Which-dimension-is-halved","page":"Real data","text":"The half spectrum is taken along the first dimension of the region. To halve dimension 2 of a matrix, put it first in the region:\n\nZ = randn(64, 128)\nsize(rfft(Z, (2, 1)))\n(33, 128)","title":"Which dimension is halved"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/real/#Plans","page":"Real data","text":"plan_rfft, plan_irfft and plan_brfft return plans with the same rules as the complex ones, including inv for the normalised inverse. Use them when the same real size is transformed many times; see Plans and reuse.","title":"Plans"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/contribute/#Contribute","page":"Contribute","text":"FFTL is meant to be developed by the Julia community, as the FFT the ecosystem shares. Help is most useful where one developer with two machines cannot reach:\n\nMeasurements on other hardware. FFTL is tuned on an Apple M1 Max, AMD EPYC (Zen 2 and 3), and an NVIDIA RTX A6000. Results on other CPUs and GPUs, and the cases where FFTL is slow or wrong there, show where the rules need work.\nApplications. A transform, number type or memory layout your code needs and FFTL does not offer.\nKernels, tuning, documentation and tests.\n\nOpen an issue with the case (size, type, transform, machine, the numbers), or a pull request. Every change is checked against the tests, and every claim about speed against a measurement. This page shows how to set up, what to run, and what a change must come with.","title":"Contribute"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/contribute/#Set-up","page":"Contribute","text":"git clone https://github.com/JuliaMath/FFTL.jl\ncd FFTL.jl\njulia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\n\nWork on a branch and open a pull request against main. Commit messages are in English and describe what the code does now, not how it got there.","title":"Set up"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/contribute/#Run-the-tests","page":"Contribute","text":"using Pkg; Pkg.test(\"FFTL\")\n\nThis runs a quick selection that covers every feature, in about 1.5 minutes. Two environment variables change it:\n\nvariable effect\nFFTL_TEST_FULL=true the full lists of sizes, kinds and element types (weekly in CI)\nFFTL_TEST_VERBOSE=true the time of every test set\n\nThe test files: runtests.jl (transforms against a reference DFT, both precisions, all kinds), ka.jl (KernelAbstractions plans, on the CPU backend), inference.jl (type inference of the public functions), radix_table.jl (the radix choice), and aqua.jl (package quality checks). CI runs the quick tests on Linux and macOS for pushes to main and for pull requests, and the full lists and Julia 1.10 once a week. The C library has its own CI job.","title":"Run the tests"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/contribute/#Build-the-documentation","page":"Contribute","text":"julia --project=docs -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --project=docs docs/make.jl\n\nThe output is in docs/build. Every Literate example in the getting started page and in the tutorials runs during the build, so a broken example fails the build.","title":"Build the documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/contribute/#Changes-to-performance","page":"Contribute","text":"A change that claims to be faster comes with an A/B measurement on the same machine, in one process per variant, with the same inputs. Report the medians of several interleaved runs and the spread between two runs of the same code: a difference inside that spread is no difference. The Performance page describes the protocol.","title":"Changes to performance"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/contribute/#Where-things-are","page":"Contribute","text":"file what it does\nsrc/codegen.jl the symbolic generator of the straight-line codelets\nsrc/kernels.jl, src/complexvec.jl codelets on SIMD vectors, the complex arithmetic\nsrc/factor.jl the radix sequence chosen for a length\nsrc/plan.jl, src/rchain.jl Stockham chains and Bluestein's algorithm; rchain.jl is the form that libfftl compiles\nsrc/threads.jl threads, lane blocks, the four-step algorithm\nsrc/interface.jl the complex plans and the public functions\nsrc/real.jl real transforms (r2c, c2r)\nsrc/r2r.jl the real-to-real kinds\nsrc/rreal.jl run-time real plans, the form that libfftl compiles\nsrc/generic.jl the path for other number types\nsrc/ka.jl, ext/ GPU through KernelAbstractions\nsrc/precompile.jl the precompile workload\nsrc/limits.jl, src/tuning.jl cache sizes and thresholds\nlibfftl/ the C library\n\nThe thresholds in src/tuning.jl are numbers measured on two machines. A change to them needs a measurement on at least one machine, reported with the change.","title":"Where things are"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/contribute/#Licensing","page":"Contribute","text":"FFTL is MIT-licensed. Code in a contribution must be your own or come from a source under a permissive license (MIT, BSD, Apache), with the notice kept. Do not copy or translate code from GPL-licensed FFT implementations, such as FFTW or KFR. Algorithms published in papers can be implemented from the paper.","title":"Licensing"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/libfftl/#libfftl-C-API","page":"libfftl C API","text":"The complete header is fftl.h. This page lists the functions and the limits; the header has the exact contracts.","title":"libfftl C API"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/libfftl/#Functions","page":"libfftl C API","text":"function purpose\nfftl_version(buf, len) the library version as \"major.minor.patch\"\nfftl_plan_dft(rank, dims, transform_rank, region, precision, sign, threads) complex DFT plan, sign −1 forward or +1 backward\nfftl_plan_dft_r2c(rank, dims, transform_rank, region, precision, threads) real input, half spectrum out; in place uses FFTW's padded real layout\nfftl_plan_dft_c2r(rank, dims, transform_rank, region, precision, threads) half spectrum in, real output, unnormalised; in place uses FFTW's padded real layout\nfftl_plan_r2r(rank, dims, transform_rank, region, kinds, precision, threads) real-to-real transforms, one kind per dimension\nfftl_plan_execute(plan, out, in) apply a plan of any of the four kinds; out == in runs in place\nfftl_plan_destroy(plan) free a plan\nfftl_last_error(buf, len) the message of the last failed call on this thread\n\nThe kinds are FFTL_REDFT00 … FFTL_REDFT11 (DCT-I to DCT-IV), FFTL_RODFT00 … FFTL_RODFT11 (DST-I to DST-IV), FFTL_DHT, FFTL_R2HC and FFTL_HC2R.","title":"Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/libfftl/#Limits","page":"libfftl C API","text":" limit\nrank any, at least 1\ndimension lengths any, at least 1; lengths with large prime factors use Bluestein's algorithm\ntransform rank 0 to the rank; the region is strictly ascending, 0-based\nprecision FFTL_PRECISION_DOUBLE (Complex{Float64}, 16 bytes) and FFTL_PRECISION_SINGLE (Complex{Float32}, 8 bytes)\nsign −1 or +1 for complex plans\nnormalisation none; the backward transform multiplies by the number of transformed elements\nthreads the value is stored per plan; the worker pool is fixed when the library is built (FFTL_THREADS). More threads than workers run on the workers that exist\nmemory bounded by the available memory and by 64-bit element offsets\nconcurrency different plans execute in parallel; one plan on one thread at a time; destroying a plan during its execution is not allowed\nnumber types float and double only; no GPU, no generic number types","title":"Limits"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/libfftl/#Build-and-size","page":"libfftl C API","text":"On macOS (arm64), the build output in libfftl/build/lib is 23 MB: the library (14 MB) and the Julia runtime next to it (10 MB). No Julia installation is needed to run a program that links it. The build needs Julia 1.12 or newer with juliac. See C library (libfftl) for the build variables.","title":"Build and size"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/libfftl/#Errors","page":"libfftl C API","text":"Constructors return a negative handle on failure; fftl_plan_execute and fftl_plan_destroy return −1 on a bad handle or a failed execution. The reason is in fftl_last_error, which keeps its message until the next failed call on the same thread.","title":"Errors"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/dft/#The-discrete-Fourier-transform","page":"The discrete Fourier transform","text":"A sequence x_0  x_n-1 of complex numbers has the discrete Fourier transform (DFT)\n\nX_k = sum_j=0^n-1 x_j e^-2pi i jkn qquad k = 0 dots n-1\n\nX_k measures how strongly the sequence correlates with the complex exponential of frequency k cycles per record. The inverse maps back:\n\nx_j = frac1n sum_k=0^n-1 X_k e^+2pi i jkn\n\nBoth sums are matrix-vector products with the n  n matrix F_kj = e^-2pi i jkn. The matrix costs n^2 multiplications, which is the naive algorithm. The point of the FFT is that F factors into sparse pieces, so the product costs O(n log n).","title":"The discrete Fourier transform"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/dft/#Conventions","page":"The discrete Fourier transform","text":"FFTL uses the two signs and the two normalisations that are common in signal processing:\n\nfunction sign normalisation\nfft - (forward) none\nbfft + (backward) none\nifft + 1n per transformed dimension\n\nWith these, textifft(textfft(x)) = x, and textbfft(textfft(x)) = nx. FFTW.jl uses the same names and the same scalings. The dct of FFTL is the orthonormal one, the convention that keeps the energy unchanged.","title":"Conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/dft/#Why-a-Fourier-transform-at-all","page":"The discrete Fourier transform","text":"For a signal sampled at rate f_s with n samples, bin k is the frequency k f_s  n. A tone that is not exactly on a bin spreads over several bins. This is the leakage that windows trade off against resolution; the spectrum tutorial uses a record that contains whole cycles, so none occurs.\n\nThe bins above n2 are the negative frequencies. For real input X_n-k = overlineX_k, so the spectrum of a real signal carries only half its information, and rfft returns that half.\n\nMultiplying spectra is convolution in time: the cyclic convolution of two sequences is the inverse transform of the product of their transforms. A linear convolution is the cyclic one of zero-padded inputs. This turns filtering of long signals into O(n log n) work; the convolution tutorial shows it.","title":"Why a Fourier transform at all"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/dft/#The-algorithm-as-divide-and-conquer","page":"The discrete Fourier transform","text":"The idea that makes the FFT fast is the one behind merge sort. Split the sum by the parity of the index, j = 2m and j = 2m+1:\n\nX_k = sum_m x_2m e^-2pi i mk(n2) + e^-2pi i kn sum_m x_2m+1 e^-2pi i mk(n2)\n\nEach sum is a DFT of length n2, and the two are combined with n multiplications by the twiddle factors e^-2pi i kn. Recursing on the halves gives T(n) = 2T(n2) + O(n), so T(n) = O(n log n). The same identity works for any factor n = r cdot m, not only 2. Radix r splits into a stage of r-point transforms. The algorithms explanation describes how FFTL turns this recursion into a sequence of loops over memory without recursion.","title":"The algorithm as divide and conquer"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/precompile/#Precompilation","page":"Precompilation","text":"The first plan for a new radix sequence compiles its kernels. Without preparation, that takes seconds for a size nobody has used before, which matters in short scripts, in a notebook that restarts, or in a service that starts and must answer quickly.\n\nFFTL ships a precompile workload that runs common plans and transforms while the package is precompiled, so the compiled code is part of the package image. It is selected with a preference:\n\nusing Preferences, FFTL\nPreferences.set_preferences!(FFTL, \"precompile_workload\" => \"broad\"; force = true)\n\nThe next load recompiles FFTL with the new setting. The values are:\n\nvalue content\n\"lean\" (default) lengths 1024, 1000 and 1009 in one dimension, 16 × 16 and 8 × 8 × 8, both element types, complex transforms, real transforms and plan_pass\n\"broad\" lengths 10⁵, 2²⁰, 128, 360, 243, the sizes 256 × 256 and 64 × 64 × 64, and a four-step case with four threads\n\"none\" no workload: smallest image, compilation at the first use\n\nThe workload applies plans and runs transforms, not just constructs them, because most of the compiled code is generated for the transform itself. A plan of a size the workload did not cover still compiles its own kernels when first used; the workload only moves that cost to package precompilation.\n\nFFTL.PRECOMPILE_WORKLOAD reports the value the package was built with.\n\nThe trade-off is size against latency. On an Apple M1 Max, the lean workload took about 32 s to precompile and makes the package image about 96 MB. The first plan and transform of a covered size then take milliseconds instead of seconds. Choose \"none\" when the image size matters more than the first call, for example in continuous integration.","title":"Precompilation"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/trim/#Compiling-ahead-of-time","page":"Compiling ahead of time","text":"juliac --trim (Julia 1.12 and newer) compiles a Julia program into an executable or a library that contains only the code the program can reach. Every call must be resolvable when the program is compiled. The normal FFTL plans do not meet this: their type contains the radix sequence, which is computed from the size at run time, so constructing a plan builds a new type.\n\nFor trimmed programs, FFTL has plans whose radix sequences and sizes are data:\n\nplan transform applied with\nFFTL.RPlan{T}(sz, region, σ) complex DFT, sign σ mul!, *, \\, or FFTL.execute! on pointers\nFFTL.RRFFTPlan{T}(sz, region, σ) r2c (σ = -1) or unnormalised c2r (σ = +1) FFTL.execute_r2c!, FFTL.execute_c2r! on pointers\nFFTL.RR2RPlan{T}(sz, kinds, region) real-to-real, one kind per dimension FFTL.execute_r2r! on pointers\n\nT is Float32 or Float64; sizes, regions and kinds are vectors. All three take the threads keyword. They compute the same transforms with the same strategies as the normal plans (Bluestein, four-step, fused planes); per stage they dispatch at run time, which costs a little speed. libfftl is built from exactly these plans, so they are the tested route through juliac --trim=safe.\n\nusing FFTL, LinearAlgebra\n\nx = randn(ComplexF64, 48, 30)\np = FFTL.RPlan{Float64}([48, 30], [1, 2], -1)\ny = similar(x)\nmul!(y, p, x)\ny ≈ fft(x)\ntrue\n\nr = randn(16)\nq = FFTL.RR2RPlan{Float64}([16], [FFTL.REDFT10], [1])\nout = similar(r)\nGC.@preserve r out FFTL.execute_r2r!(q, pointer(out), pointer(r))\nout ≈ FFTL.r2r(r, FFTL.REDFT10)\ntrue\n\nThe pointer functions expect column-major arrays of the plan's size, which the caller keeps alive (GC.@preserve). The source of libfftl (libfftl/src/libfftl.jl) shows the rules the calling code follows to stay trimmable: no closures that capture types, concrete element types at every call, error messages as literal strings.","title":"Compiling ahead of time"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/fftw/#Coming-from-FFTW.jl","page":"Coming from FFTW.jl","text":"using FFTL can take the place of using FFTW. FFTL exports the names FFTW.jl exports, with the same signs, scalings and keywords: the DFT functions fft, bfft, ifft, rfft, brfft, irfft, the orthonormal dct and idct, their plan_ and in-place forms, and AbstractFFTs's helpers (fftshift, fftfreq, …). As in FFTW.jl, FFTL.r2r, the real-to-real kinds (FFTL.REDFT10, …), the flags and FFTL.set_num_threads are used with the prefix; the kinds have FFTW's names and FFTW's unnormalised definitions.","title":"Coming from FFTW.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/fftw/#The-same-transforms-under-the-same-names","page":"Coming from FFTW.jl","text":"FFTW.jl FFTL remark\nfft, bfft, ifft, rfft, brfft, irfft and their plans the same same signs and scalings\nfft!, plan_fft!, … the same the plans overwrite their argument\ndct, idct, plan_dct, dct!, … the same orthonormal DCT-II and its inverse\nFFTW.r2r(x, FFTW.REDFT10) FFTL.r2r(x, FFTL.REDFT10) all eleven kinds, same definitions\nplan_fft(x; flags = FFTW.MEASURE, timelimit = 10) plan_fft(x; flags = FFTL.MEASURE, timelimit = 10) accepted, without effect (see below)\nplan_fft(x; num_threads = 4) the same, or threads = 4 \nFFTW.set_num_threads(n) FFTL.set_num_threads(n) default for the plans made afterwards\nwisdom (export_wisdom, import_wisdom) not needed plans do not measure\n\nThe comparison runs with both packages loaded, so the names are qualified; FFTW.jl is not a dependency of FFTL, so the example is shown, not run:\n\nusing FFTW, FFTL\n\nx = randn(12, 7)\nFFTL.dct(x) ≈ FFTW.dct(x)                                     # true\nFFTL.r2r(x, FFTL.RODFT11, 1) ≈ FFTW.r2r(x, FFTW.RODFT11, 1)   # true, likewise for every kind\n\nThe test suite runs such comparisons for every transform, plan form and kind in both precisions.","title":"The same transforms under the same names"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/fftw/#Planning-without-flags","page":"Coming from FFTW.jl","text":"FFTW's planner can measure candidate algorithms at run time (MEASURE, PATIENT) and keep the result as wisdom. FFTL has no such step: the radix sequence of a length comes from a fixed rule that was derived from offline measurements, and the codelets are compiled for exactly that sequence. A plan therefore gives the same algorithm in every session; the flags and timelimit keywords are accepted so that FFTW.jl code runs unchanged, and have no effect. The cost that remains is compilation: the first plan of a new radix sequence compiles its kernels, which Precompilation moves to package installation for the common sizes.","title":"Planning without flags"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/fftw/#FFTL-and-FFTW.jl-in-one-session","page":"Coming from FFTW.jl","text":"fft, plan_fft, dct, … are FFTL's own functions. A module that writes using FFTL gets FFTL, also when a package it depends on loads FFTW.jl, and that package keeps getting FFTW. In a module that loads both, the unqualified names are ambiguous and Julia asks for the prefix: FFTL.fft(x) or FFTW.fft(x).\n\nCode that calls AbstractFFTs.jl's generic functions (AbstractFFTs.fft, or fft after using AbstractFFTs) also runs on FFTL. With FFTW.jl loaded, those generic functions run on FFTW for StridedArrays of Float32 and Float64 values, FFTW's own domain, and on FFTL for the rest, for example BigFloat arrays. Since AbstractFFTs exports the same names, a module that wants FFTL's functions without the prefix writes import AbstractFFTs rather than using AbstractFFTs next to using FFTL.","title":"FFTL and FFTW.jl in one session"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/fftw/#The-generic-interface","page":"Coming from FFTW.jl","text":"Every FFTL plan is an AbstractFFTs.Plan and supports size, ndims, fftdims, mul!(y, p, x), p * x, inv(p) and p \\ y. Code written against AbstractFFTs.Plan accepts an FFTL plan:\n\nusing FFTL, LinearAlgebra\nimport AbstractFFTs\n\nfunction roundtrip(p::AbstractFFTs.Plan, x)\n  y = p * x\n  p \\ y\nend\n\nx = randn(ComplexF64, 32, 8)\np = plan_fft(x)\nroundtrip(p, x) ≈ x\ntrue","title":"The generic interface"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/fftw/#What-FFTL-adds","page":"Coming from FFTW.jl","text":"Number types beyond Float32 and Float64, computed in their own precision (Number types).\nGPU arrays with the same functions (GPU).\nPasses over one dimension of a strided array, with pruning (Transforms along dimensions).\nA C library compiled from the Julia code (libfftl).","title":"What FFTL adds"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/algorithms/#Algorithms","page":"Algorithms","text":"This page goes from the mathematics of the DFT to what FFTL runs. Each transform in the library is built from a few pieces, and the same pieces appear in the complex, real and real-to-real paths.","title":"Algorithms"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/algorithms/#Stockham-chains","page":"Algorithms","text":"A transform of length n = r_1 r_2 cdots r_s is a sequence of s stages. Stage t combines r_t interleaved sub-transforms with twiddle factors and writes its result to a second buffer, and the two buffers swap roles at each stage. This is the Stockham formulation, which keeps the data in natural order throughout:\n\nno bit reversal or digit reversal at the start or the end,\neach stage reads and writes with regular strides,\nthe radix sequence r_1  r_s is a single object that the plan carries.\n\nThe radices are chosen by a fixed rule (src/factor.jl). The rule is the result of exhaustive searches over the decompositions of many lengths on the development machines, not of a runtime search. It prefers radix 4 and radix 8 for powers of two. On aarch64 it merges a factor 3 with a 4 into a 12; the x86 rules differ and are in the same file. Every other prime up to 31 is a radix of its own. Larger primes are not factored; they go to Bluestein's algorithm.","title":"Stockham chains"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/algorithms/#Codelets","page":"Algorithms","text":"A stage of radix r is a small DFT applied to r values at once. For each r the codelet is straight-line code without loops, generated by a symbolic generator (src/codegen.jl). The generator writes the DFT as a sum of terms and simplifies it:\n\nmultiplications by pm i are rotations of the term, not multiplications; two terms with rotations that differ by an odd power of i combine in one complex add-and-rotate;\na composite radix r = r_1 r_2 is split in registers with the usual index mapping;\nan odd prime uses the symmetric form: X_k X_r-k = A  i B with the cosine sums A and the sine sums B, which pairs the outputs k and r-k.\n\nThe generated code runs on complex SIMD vectors (src/complexvec.jl), with the vector width of the element type. On aarch64 the complex add-and-rotate and the complex multiply are single instructions (FCADD, FCMLA).","title":"Codelets"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/algorithms/#Pruned-codelets","page":"Algorithms","text":"When the input is known to be zero outside some range, or only some outputs are used, the generator drops the terms that are zero and the statements that feed no needed output. A pass that transforms a zero-padded array therefore does not read the padding, and an NFFT that needs only part of the spectrum does not compute the rest. See transforms along dimensions.","title":"Pruned codelets"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/algorithms/#Bluestein's-algorithm","page":"Algorithms","text":"Lengths with a prime factor above 31 go through Bluestein's identity. With jk = (j^2 + k^2 - (k-j)^2)2,\n\nX_k = c_k sum_j (x_j c_j) overlinec_k-j qquad c_j = e^sigma pi i j^2n\n\nThe sum is a cyclic convolution with a kernel that depends only on n. Its length M is a smooth number with M ge 2n - 1, so the convolution is two transforms of smooth lengths and one pointwise product. The kernel's transform is computed once when the plan is made.\n\nPer line, Bluestein costs two transforms of length M and a pointwise product, so it is used only for lengths that the smooth codelets cannot cover. On aarch64 the convolution length is the smallest 5-smooth number with M ge 2n - 1. On x86 a cost model fitted to measured chains picks the fastest 7-smooth length in 2n - 1 14(2n - 1): a length with many radix 9, 5 or 3 stages transforms several times slower than a power of two a few percent larger (src/plan.jl).","title":"Bluestein's algorithm"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/algorithms/#Multidimensional-transforms-without-transposition","page":"Algorithms","text":"A transform along dimension d of an array with strides S_d is a set of lines with stride S_d. FFTL does not copy the data into lines. The S_d adjacent lines of a dimension with stride S_d are processed together, one SIMD lane per line, so no transposition is needed. Dimensions 1 and 2 are fused per plane when the plane fits in cache.","title":"Multidimensional transforms without transposition"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/algorithms/#Long-one-dimensional-transforms:-four-step","page":"Algorithms","text":"A single long line with a smooth length n = n_1 n_2 on several threads uses the four-step algorithm:\n\nn_2 transforms of length n_1 over the columns,\nmultiplication by the twiddle factors e^sigma 2pi i j_1 k_2n,\nn_1 transforms of length n_2 over the rows.\n\nEach of the two transform steps is split over the threads, with a barrier in between. The factorisation n_1 n_2 is chosen when the plan is made (fourstep_split).","title":"Long one-dimensional transforms: four-step"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/algorithms/#Real-transforms","page":"Algorithms","text":"For even n = 2M, a real line is read as M complex values z_j = x_2j + ix_2j+1, which is the memory layout as it is. One complex DFT of length M then gives the half spectrum:\n\nX_k = tfrac12bigl(Z_k + overlineZ_M-kbigr) - tfraci2 e^-2pi i knbigl(Z_k - overlineZ_M-kbigr)\n\nThe pairs k and M-k share their products, so the work is half of a complex transform of length n. Odd lengths use a complex DFT of length n directly. The inverse (c2r) runs the same steps backwards.","title":"Real transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/algorithms/#Real-to-real-transforms","page":"Algorithms","text":"Each kind is computed from a real or complex DFT of a rearranged input. Examples:\n\nDCT-II. The even-indexed inputs go forward and the odd-indexed ones backward: v = (x_0 x_2 x_4 dots x_5 x_3 x_1). A real DFT of v, multiplied by e^-ipi k(2n), gives the DCT-II. This is the reordering that makes the DCT cost one real DFT of length n.\nDST-II and DST-IV are DCTs of the input with alternating signs, read in reverse order.\nDCT-I and DST-I extend the input to an even or odd sequence of length 2(n-1) or 2(n+1) and take one real DFT of that length.\nDHT is the real DFT with the real and imaginary parts combined: operatornameRe F_k - operatornameIm F_k.\n\nThe formulas are in the header comment of src/r2r.jl. Their inverses and scale factors are listed on the cosine, sine and Hartley page.","title":"Real-to-real transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/algorithms/#Passes","page":"Algorithms","text":"plan_pass applies one 1D transform to all lines of a strided array with the same kernel. It lets a library assemble its own multidimensional transform one dimension at a time and declare, per dimension, which inputs are zero and which outputs it needs; both declarations use the pruned codelets above. NFFT.jl transforms its oversampled grid this way.","title":"Passes"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/libfftl_header/#C-header-(fftl.h)","page":"C header (fftl.h)","text":"/* libfftl: FFTL.jl as a C library.\n *\n * Plans by opaque handle for the complex DFT, the real-input DFT (r2c) and its inverse (c2r),\n * and real-to-real transforms (DCT, DST, Hartley, halfcomplex): any rank, any lengths, both\n * precisions, single- or multi-threaded execution. The plan is built, applied\n * and freed through the four calls below; everything else about it is run-time data inside the\n * library (radix sequence, algorithm and work buffers are chosen and owned by FFTL).\n *\n * Data layout: complex values are interleaved pairs (re, im), stored contiguously in\n * column-major order: in a dims[0] x ... x dims[rank-1] array the first index varies fastest,\n * so the value (i1, ..., ir) sits at offset i1 + dims[0] * (i2 + dims[1] * (...)). Buffers need\n * no alignment beyond what malloc returns, and out-of-place execution (out != in) may not\n * overlap partially.\n *\n * Precision: the element type (8 or 16 bytes per complex value) is fixed by the plan; buffer\n * pointers are passed as void * and must point to the matching layout.\n *\n * Normalization: nothing is normalised. The backward transform is the plain unnormalised\n * inverse map: applying the sign +1 plan after the sign -1 plan multiplies the input by the\n * number of transformed elements.\n */\n#ifndef FFTL_H\n#define FFTL_H\n\n#include <stddef.h>\n#include <stdint.h>\n\n#ifdef __cplusplus\nextern \"C\" {\n#endif\n\nenum {\n  FFTL_PRECISION_DOUBLE = 1,   /* Complex{Float64}, 16 bytes per value */\n  FFTL_PRECISION_SINGLE = 2   /* Complex{Float32},  8 bytes per value */\n};\n\n/* Real-to-real kinds (fftl_plan_r2r), unnormalised, of a line x_0 … x_(n-1):\n *   FFTL_REDFT00  DCT-I    y_k = x_0 + (-1)^k x_(n-1) + 2 Σ_(j=1)^(n-2) x_j cos(πjk/(n-1)), n ≥ 2\n *   FFTL_REDFT10  DCT-II   y_k = 2 Σ x_j cos(π(j+½)k/n)\n *   FFTL_REDFT01  DCT-III  y_k = x_0 + 2 Σ_(j≥1) x_j cos(πj(k+½)/n)\n *   FFTL_REDFT11  DCT-IV   y_k = 2 Σ x_j cos(π(j+½)(k+½)/n)\n *   FFTL_RODFT00  DST-I    y_k = 2 Σ x_j sin(π(j+1)(k+1)/(n+1))\n *   FFTL_RODFT10  DST-II   y_k = 2 Σ x_j sin(π(j+½)(k+1)/n)\n *   FFTL_RODFT01  DST-III  y_k = (-1)^k x_(n-1) + 2 Σ_(j≤n-2) x_j sin(π(j+1)(k+½)/n)\n *   FFTL_RODFT11  DST-IV   y_k = 2 Σ x_j sin(π(j+½)(k+½)/n)\n *   FFTL_DHT      Hartley  y_k = Σ x_j (cos(2πjk/n) + sin(2πjk/n))\n *   FFTL_R2HC     DFT F_k = Σ x_j e^(-2πijk/n) in halfcomplex order: y_k = Re F_k for\n *                 0 ≤ k ≤ n/2, y_(n-k) = Im F_k for 0 < k < n/2\n *   FFTL_HC2R     inverse of FFTL_R2HC: y_j = Σ_k F_k e^(2πijk/n), F in halfcomplex order\n * Inverse pairs (up to the factor 2n, 2(n-1) for DCT-I, 2(n+1) for DST-I, n for DHT and the\n * halfcomplex pair): DCT-II/III, DST-II/III; DCT-I, DCT-IV, DST-I, DST-IV, DHT are their own\n * inverses. */\nenum {\n  FFTL_REDFT00 = 0, FFTL_REDFT10 = 1, FFTL_REDFT01 = 2, FFTL_REDFT11 = 3,\n  FFTL_RODFT00 = 4, FFTL_RODFT10 = 5, FFTL_RODFT01 = 6, FFTL_RODFT11 = 7,\n  FFTL_DHT = 8, FFTL_R2HC = 9, FFTL_HC2R = 10\n};\n\n/* Plans are opaque handles. A negative handle is an error and fftl_last_error explains;\n * the only valid operations on an invalid handle are fftl_last_error and passing it to\n * fftl_plan_destroy and fftl_plan_execute, which reject it. */\ntypedef int64_t fftl_plan;\n\n/* size_t fftl_version(char *buf, size_t buf_len)\n *\n * Writes the library version as a zero-terminated \"major.minor.patch\" string into buf (at\n * most buf_len bytes including the terminator) and returns the full length of the version\n * string, like snprintf. With buf == NULL it only returns the length.\n */\nsize_t fftl_version(char *buf, size_t buf_len);\n\n/* fftl_plan fftl_plan_dft(int32_t rank, const int64_t *dims, int32_t transform_rank,\n *                         const int32_t *region, int32_t precision, int32_t sign,\n *                         int32_t threads)\n *\n * Plan for the complex DFT of a rank-dimensional array of dims[0] x ... x dims[rank-1]\n * complex values along the dimensions region[0] ... region[transform_rank-1], with the given\n * precision (FFTL_PRECISION_*) and sign (-1 forward, +1 backward, unnormalised).\n *\n * rank                 >= 1: the rank of the array the buffers point to; dims has this\n *                      length. There is no upper limit; memory and the 64-bit element\n *                      offsets bound the product of the dimensions in practice.\n * dims                 the array dimensions, each >= 1; any length is planned (lengths with\n *                      large prime factors go through Bluestein's convolution algorithm,\n *                      smooth lengths through radix stages).\n * transform_rank       0 to rank: the length of region. Zero transforms nothing; executing\n *                      such a plan just copies input to output (in place: no-op).\n * region               transformed dimensions as indices into dims: 0-based (0 is the\n *                      fastest-varying index), strictly ascending, each within 0 ... rank-1.\n *                      Duplicates and out-of-range indices are errors, as are region arrays\n *                      that are not strictly ascending.\n * sign                 -1 or +1.\n * threads              number of worker threads for execution, >= 1. The plan stores it;\n *                      execution uses the plan's value, bounded by what the transform size\n *                      allows (small transforms run on one thread regardless).\n *\n * The thread count of the library's own worker pool is fixed when the library is built (see\n * \"Threading\" at the end of this file); a plan with more threads than that queues its work\n * on fewer workers, which gives no speedup.\n *\n * Returns an fftl_plan, or -1 with the reason in fftl_last_error: unsupported precision or\n * sign, rank or transform_rank or region invalid, an array dimension below one, or out of\n * memory.\n */\nfftl_plan fftl_plan_dft(int32_t rank, const int64_t *dims, int32_t transform_rank,\n                        const int32_t *region, int32_t precision, int32_t sign,\n                        int32_t threads);\n\n/* fftl_plan fftl_plan_dft_r2c(int32_t rank, const int64_t *dims, int32_t transform_rank,\n *                             const int32_t *region, int32_t precision, int32_t threads)\n * fftl_plan fftl_plan_dft_c2r(int32_t rank, const int64_t *dims, int32_t transform_rank,\n *                             const int32_t *region, int32_t precision, int32_t threads)\n *\n * Real-input forward DFT (r2c, sign -1) and its unnormalised inverse (c2r, sign +1) of a real\n * array of dims[0] x ... x dims[rank-1] values along region (as in fftl_plan_dft;\n * transform_rank >= 1). dims are the sizes of the REAL array in both cases. The complex array\n * holds the half spectrum: along the first transformed dimension d = region[0] it has\n * dims[d] / 2 + 1 values, the other dimensions as in dims. r2c reads the real array and\n * writes the complex one; c2r reads the complex array (and leaves it unchanged) and writes\n * the real one; c2r after r2c multiplies by the number of transformed elements.\n *\n * In place (out == in, as in FFTW): the real array is padded along d to 2 * (dims[d] / 2 + 1)\n * values, so that it occupies exactly the memory of the complex half spectrum. Its values at\n * positions dims[d] and above along d are ignored by r2c and undefined after c2r; the in-place\n * c2r overwrites its input.\n */\nfftl_plan fftl_plan_dft_r2c(int32_t rank, const int64_t *dims, int32_t transform_rank,\n                            const int32_t *region, int32_t precision, int32_t threads);\nfftl_plan fftl_plan_dft_c2r(int32_t rank, const int64_t *dims, int32_t transform_rank,\n                            const int32_t *region, int32_t precision, int32_t threads);\n\n/* fftl_plan fftl_plan_r2r(int32_t rank, const int64_t *dims, int32_t transform_rank,\n *                         const int32_t *region, const int32_t *kinds, int32_t precision,\n *                         int32_t threads)\n *\n * Real-to-real transforms of a real array along region (as in fftl_plan_dft), kinds[i]\n * (FFTL_REDFT00 … FFTL_HC2R) along dimension region[i]. Input and output are real arrays of\n * size dims; in place (out == in) or out of place.\n */\nfftl_plan fftl_plan_r2r(int32_t rank, const int64_t *dims, int32_t transform_rank,\n                        const int32_t *region, const int32_t *kinds, int32_t precision,\n                        int32_t threads);\n\n/* int32_t fftl_plan_execute(fftl_plan p, void *out, const void *in)\n *\n * Executes the plan of any of the four kinds: transforms the elements of in according to the\n * plan's region and stores the result in out. out and in point to separate arrays (out of\n * place) or the same pointer (in place): fftl_plan_execute(p, buf, buf); out and in may not\n * partially overlap. r2c/c2r plans in place use the padded real layout described at\n * fftl_plan_dft_r2c. The buffers hold the\n * arrays described at the plan's creation (complex or real values in the plan's precision).\n *\n * Returns 0, or -1 if the handle is invalid (the buffers are untouched) or the execution failed\n * (out of memory; out is then undefined); fftl_last_error has the reason.\n */\nint32_t fftl_plan_execute(fftl_plan p, void *out, const void *in);\n\n/* int32_t fftl_plan_destroy(fftl_plan p)\n *\n * Frees the plan and its tables and buffers. The handle must not be used afterwards; passing\n * it again is rejected as an invalid handle. Destroying is not synchronised with a concurrent\n * execution of the same plan (see \"Threading\" below).\n */\nint32_t fftl_plan_destroy(fftl_plan p);\n\n/* size_t fftl_last_error(char *buf, size_t buf_len)\n *\n * Writes the message of the last failed call of this thread as a zero-terminated string into\n * buf (at most buf_len bytes including the terminator) and returns the full length of the\n * message, like snprintf. An empty message means no error so far. With buf == NULL it only\n * returns the length. Successful calls do not clear the message.\n */\nsize_t fftl_last_error(char *buf, size_t buf_len);\n\n/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * Threading\n *\n * libfftl embeds the Julia runtime, whose worker pool is fixed when the library is BUILT and\n * cannot change afterwards: build.sh bakes the number through juliac's --jl-option threads\n * (its FFTL_THREADS variable: a number, or \"auto\" for one worker per CPU of the machine the\n * library is LOADED on, counted at load time from the process's CPU affinity; the default is\n * \"auto\", on macOS the number of performance cores of the build machine). JULIA_NUM_THREADS\n * in the environment of the loading process has no effect: the baked option takes precedence.\n * The Julia runtime cannot grow its pool after it starts, so changing the count needs a\n * rebuild.\n *\n * A plan's threads value above the baked worker count is allowed and correct: execution\n * queues that plan's tasks on the available workers, which gives no speedup - and neither\n * does a baked count above the number of cores the process may use. The thread that loads\n * the library is one of the workers: a runtime baked with 4 runs a threads = 4 plan on all\n * 4, while the calling thread waits for them inside fftl_plan_execute.\n *\n * All entry points may be called from the loading thread (and, with the usual care for\n * embedded runtimes, from other host threads that the Julia runtime knows about; the plain\n * case is a single caller thread). Plans are created and destroyed under the library's\n * registry lock; a single plan must not execute on two threads at the same time; different\n * plans can run in parallel. Destroying a plan while it executes is not allowed (the\n * executing thread keeps its own reference until fftl_plan_execute returns, but the handle\n * becomes invalid).\n * ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n\n#ifdef __cplusplus\n}\n#endif\n\n#endif /* FFTL_H */","title":"C header (fftl.h)"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/cosine_sine/#Cosine,-sine-and-Hartley-transforms","page":"Cosine, sine and Hartley transforms","text":"Real-to-real transforms map real data to real data. They are the transforms behind JPEG (DCT-II), spectral Poisson solvers with Dirichlet or Neumann boundary conditions (DST-I, DCT-I), and the Hartley transform. FFTL computes all eleven kinds, along any dimensions, each dimension with its own kind.","title":"Cosine, sine and Hartley transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/cosine_sine/#The-kinds","page":"Cosine, sine and Hartley transforms","text":"The formulas are unnormalised. n is the length along the transformed dimension.\n\nconstant kind y_k\nREDFT00 DCT-I x_0 + (-1)^k x_n-1 + 2sum_j=1^n-2 x_j cos(pi jk(n-1))\nREDFT10 DCT-II 2sum_j x_j cos(pi(j+tfrac12)kn)\nREDFT01 DCT-III x_0 + 2sum_jge1 x_j cos(pi j(k+tfrac12)n)\nREDFT11 DCT-IV 2sum_j x_j cos(pi(j+tfrac12)(k+tfrac12)n)\nRODFT00 DST-I 2sum_j x_j sin(pi(j+1)(k+1)(n+1))\nRODFT10 DST-II 2sum_j x_j sin(pi(j+tfrac12)(k+1)n)\nRODFT01 DST-III (-1)^k x_n-1 + 2sum_jle n-2 x_j sin(pi(j+1)(k+tfrac12)n)\nRODFT11 DST-IV 2sum_j x_j sin(pi(j+tfrac12)(k+tfrac12)n)\nDHT Hartley sum_j x_j (cos(2pi jkn) + sin(2pi jkn))\nR2HC real DFT, halfcomplex order operatornameRe F_k for k le n2, then operatornameIm F_k\nHC2R its inverse sum_k F_k e^2pi i jkn from halfcomplex input\n\nR2HC is the DFT F_k = sum_j x_j e^-2pi i jkn of real data, stored in the same n reals as the input: the real parts for k le n2, the imaginary parts for 0  k  n2 at position n - k.","title":"The kinds"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/cosine_sine/#Pairs-and-scale-factors","page":"Cosine, sine and Hartley transforms","text":"The inverses are the same kind, or a partner kind, times a factor. For each kind, applying it twice gives the input times the factor below, so the normalised inverse divides by it:\n\nkind applied twice (or with its partner) gives\nDCT-I (REDFT00) 2(n-1)\nDST-I (RODFT00) 2(n+1)\nDCT-II / DCT-III, DST-II / DST-III, DCT-IV, DST-IV 2n\nDHT, R2HC with HC2R n\n\nThe example checks the self-inverse kinds directly:\n\nusing FFTL, LinearAlgebra\n\nn = 9\nx = [sin(0.7 * j) + 0.1 * j for j in 0:n-1]\nfor (kind, factor) in ((FFTL.REDFT00, 2(n - 1)), (FFTL.REDFT11, 2n),\n                       (FFTL.RODFT00, 2(n + 1)), (FFTL.RODFT11, 2n), (FFTL.DHT, n))\n  y = FFTL.r2r(FFTL.r2r(x, kind), kind) ./ factor\n  println(rpad(string(kind), 10), maximum(abs, y - x) < 1e-12)\nend\nREDFT00   true\nREDFT11   true\nRODFT00   true\nRODFT11   true\nDHT       true","title":"Pairs and scale factors"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/cosine_sine/#The-orthonormal-DCT-II","page":"Cosine, sine and Hartley transforms","text":"dct is the DCT-II scaled to an orthonormal map, and idct its exact inverse. It is the transform most users want for compression and for energy-preserving spectra:\n\nc = randn(16)\nD = dct(c)\n(norm(D) ≈ norm(c), idct(D) ≈ c)\n(true, true)\n\nIts values are the unnormalised DCT-II divided by 2sqrtn for the zero frequency and by sqrt2n for the others, which is the usual orthonormal scaling.\n\nR = FFTL.r2r(c, FFTL.REDFT10)\nD[1] ≈ R[1] / (2 * sqrt(16)), D[2] ≈ R[2] / sqrt(2 * 16)\n(true, true)","title":"The orthonormal DCT-II"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/cosine_sine/#Several-dimensions-with-different-kinds","page":"Cosine, sine and Hartley transforms","text":"A region takes one kind per dimension. The example uses DCT-II along the rows and the real DFT along the columns:\n\nM = randn(8, 6)\nY = FFTL.r2r(M, (FFTL.REDFT10, FFTL.R2HC), (1, 2))\nsize(Y)\n(8, 6)\n\nPlans work as for the complex transforms: plan_r2r returns a plan, inv of a plan gives its normalised inverse for the pairs above, and plan_r2r_pass transforms one dimension of a strided array.","title":"Several dimensions with different kinds"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/gpu/#GPU","page":"GPU","text":"With KernelAbstractions.jl and a GPU package loaded, the same functions accept GPU arrays. The example needs a GPU, so it is shown but not run during the documentation build.\n\nusing FFTL, Metal, KernelAbstractions  # or CUDA, AMDGPU, oneAPI\n\nxd = MtlArray(randn(ComplexF32, 1024, 1024))\nyd = fft(xd)                           # a new GPU array\np = plan_fft(xd, 2)                    # plan along dimension 2\nyd2 = similar(xd)\nmul!(yd2, p, xd)                       # out of place: yd2 !== xd\n\nRd = rfft(MtlArray(randn(Float32, 1024, 1024)))","title":"GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/gpu/#What-to-expect","page":"GPU","text":"Out of place. GPU transforms write into a new array. mul!(y, p, x) needs y !== x.\nAsynchronous. Kernels are queued on the device without waiting. Copying a result to the host waits for them. For timing, call KernelAbstractions.synchronize(backend) before reading the clock.\nThreads are ignored. The device runs the parallelism.\nPlans cost memory. A plan holds about two buffers of the array's size on the device.\nPlans compile. Making a plan for a new size generates and compiles its kernels. Make plans once and keep them, as on the CPU.\nElement types. Float32 and Float64 where the device supports Float64. Complex transforms are the tuned path.","title":"What to expect"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/gpu/#What-is-tuned","page":"GPU","text":"Complex transforms use multi-stage kernels. Each kernel computes one pass of a large radix (up to 4096 points) in workgroup memory, and a transform is one to five passes per dimension. Pass and workgroup sizes are tuned on an Apple M1 Max: there, Float32 transforms were faster than Metal.jl's FFT in nearly all measured cases. The CUDA path is tuned and measured on an NVIDIA RTX A6000 against cuFFT and VkFFT (Benchmarks).\n\nReal transforms (rfft, irfft, brfft) compute the half spectrum through a complex transform of half the length, with the pre- and post-processing inside its first and last pass.\n\nThe real-to-real kinds are reduced to complex DFTs: of half the length for DCT-II to DCT-IV and DST-II to DST-IV of even length, of length n - 1 for DCT-I and n + 1 for DST-I, mostly with the extra steps inside the passes. Odd lengths of the other kinds take a complex DFT of length 2n, the Hartley and halfcomplex kinds one of length n. DCT-II and DST-II are the most tuned of them; for the other kinds the speed relative to VkFFT depends on the kind and the size, from faster to several times slower.\n\nOther GPUs run through the same code. They are not tuned, and AMD and Intel GPUs have not been tested.","title":"What is tuned"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/gpu/#Making-plans-without-a-GPU-array","page":"GPU","text":"FFTL.plan_ka(backend, T, dims, σ; region) makes a plan for any KernelAbstractions backend, including the CPU backend, which is useful for testing GPU code without a GPU. plan_ka_rfft, plan_ka_brfft and plan_ka_r2r do the same for the real transforms.","title":"Making plans without a GPU array"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/poisson/#Solving-Poisson's-equation-with-the-DST","page":"Solving Poisson's equation with the DST","text":"Find u on the unit square with -Delta u = f and u = 0 on the boundary. Finite differences on the n  n interior grid turn this into a linear system with the five-point Laplacian. That matrix is diagonalised by the sine functions, which is exactly what the DST-I computes. So the system is solved by three steps: transform, divide by the eigenvalues, transform back.\n\nThe test problem has a known answer, u(x y) = sin(pi x)sin(2pi y), so the error is measured, not estimated. Then f = 5pi^2 u.\n\nusing FFTL, CairoMakie, LinearAlgebra\n\nThe eigenvalues of the 1D second difference with Dirichlet conditions are λ_j = (2 - 2cos(jπh))h^2, and the 2D eigenvalues are their sums, Λ_jk = λ_j + λ_k. Dividing the DST coefficients by Λ and transforming back solves the system; the scaling by (2(n+1))^2 is explained at the end.\n\nfunction poisson_solve(n)\n  h = 1 / (n + 1)\n  x = [i * h for i in 1:n]\n  uex = [sin(π * xi) * sin(2π * yj) for xi in x, yj in x]\n  f = 5π^2 .* uex\n\n  λ1 = [(2 - 2cos(j * π * h)) / h^2 for j in 1:n]\n  Λ = [λ1[j] + λ1[k] for j in 1:n, k in 1:n]\n\n  F = FFTL.r2r(f, (FFTL.RODFT00, FFTL.RODFT00), (1, 2))\n  F ./= Λ\n  u = FFTL.r2r(F, (FFTL.RODFT00, FFTL.RODFT00), (1, 2))\n  u ./= (2 * (n + 1))^2\n  u, uex\nend\n\nfunction poisson_error(n)\n  u, uex = poisson_solve(n)\n  maximum(abs, u - uex)\nend\npoisson_error (generic function with 1 method)\n\nHalving the grid spacing should divide the error by four (second order):\n\nerrs = [poisson_error(n) for n in (31, 63, 127)]\nfor (n, e) in zip((31, 63, 127), errs)\n  println(\"n = \", n, \", max error \", round(e; sigdigits = 3))\nend\nprintln(\"ratios: \", round.(errs[1:end-1] ./ errs[2:end]; digits = 2))\nn = 31, max error 0.00273\nn = 63, max error 0.000683\nn = 127, max error 0.000171\nratios: [4.0, 4.0]\n\nThe numerical solution for n = 63, next to the convergence over a wider range of grid sizes against the slope-two guide:\n\nu, uex = poisson_solve(63)\nnvec = (15, 31, 63, 127, 255)\nh = [1 / (n + 1) for n in nvec]\nerrs = [poisson_error(n) for n in nvec]\n\nfig = Figure(size = (760, 330))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1]; title = \"solution, n = 63\")\nhidedecorations!(ax1)\nheatmap!(ax1, u)\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2]; xscale = log10, yscale = log10, xlabel = \"grid spacing h\",\n           ylabel = \"max error\")\nscatter!(ax2, h, errs)\nlines!(ax2, h, errs)\nlines!(ax2, h, errs[1] .* (h ./ h[1]) .^ 2; linestyle = :dash, color = :gray,\n       label = \"slope 2\")\naxislegend(ax2; position = :rt)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Solving Poisson's equation with the DST"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/poisson/#The-scaling","page":"Solving Poisson's equation with the DST","text":"The DST-I applied twice multiplies by 2(n+1), which is why the inverse divides by (2(n+1))^2 in two dimensions. FFTL.r2r does not normalise, so this factor is part of the algorithm, not of the library. The cosine, sine and Hartley how-to lists the factor for each kind.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"The scaling"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/getting_started/#Your-first-transform","page":"Your first transform","text":"FFTL.jl computes the discrete Fourier transform (DFT) of arrays with any number of dimensions and any length. using FFTL makes the transform functions available under the names of FFTW.jl (fft, ifft, rfft, dct, … and their plan_ forms).\n\nusing FFTL, LinearAlgebra, Random\nRandom.seed!(1);","title":"Your first transform"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/getting_started/#A-transform-checked-against-the-definition","page":"Your first transform","text":"The forward transform of a vector x of length n is X_k = sum_j x_j e^-2pi i jkn. Here is that sum computed directly, next to fft:\n\nn = 8\nx = randn(ComplexF64, n);\nX_direct = [sum(x[j + 1] * cis(-2π * j * k / n) for j in 0:n-1) for k in 0:n-1]\nX = fft(x)\nnorm(X - X_direct)\n7.73360193344466e-15\n\nThe forward transform is not normalised, the inverse is: ifft(fft(x)) ≈ x. bfft is the unnormalised backward transform.\n\nifft(X) ≈ x\ntrue","title":"A transform checked against the definition"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/getting_started/#Plans","page":"Your first transform","text":"fft(x) makes a plan and applies it. A plan holds everything that depends only on the size and the dimensions: radix sequence, twiddle tables and work buffers. Make it once and apply it many times, in place or out of place:\n\np = plan_fft(x)\ny = p * x\nz = similar(x)\nmul!(z, p, x)\ny ≈ X, z ≈ X\n(true, true)\n\nPlans are AbstractFFTs.Plans, so inv(p) and p \\ y give the normalised inverse.\n\np \\ X ≈ x\ntrue","title":"Plans"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/getting_started/#Several-dimensions","page":"Your first transform","text":"An array is transformed along all its dimensions by default. A region selects some of them:\n\nA = randn(ComplexF64, 16, 12)\nsize(fft(A)), size(fft(A, 1))\n((16, 12), (16, 12))","title":"Several dimensions"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/getting_started/#Real-input","page":"Your first transform","text":"For real data, rfft returns the half spectrum: the values for frequencies 0  n2 only, since the others are their complex conjugates.\n\nr = randn(1000)\nR = rfft(r)\nsize(R), irfft(R, 1000) ≈ r\n((501,), true)","title":"Real input"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/getting_started/#Orthonormal-cosine-transform","page":"Your first transform","text":"dct is the DCT-II scaled to an orthonormal map, so it preserves the Euclidean norm and idct is its exact inverse:\n\nc = randn(100)\nD = dct(c)\nnorm(D) ≈ norm(c), idct(D) ≈ c\n(true, true)\n\nNext: the tutorials apply these to real problems, and the how-to guides explain the options.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Orthonormal cosine transform"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/dimensions/#Transforms-along-dimensions","page":"Transforms along dimensions","text":"Every function takes a region: the dimensions to transform, as an integer or a collection of integers. The default is all dimensions. Dimensions that are not in the region are batch dimensions: each slice along them is an independent transform.\n\nusing FFTL, LinearAlgebra, Random\nRandom.seed!(4)\n\nA = randn(ComplexF64, 32, 20, 6)\nsize(fft(A)), size(fft(A, 2))\n((32, 20, 6), (32, 20, 6))\n\nTransforming along dimension 2 gives the same result as transforming each slice A[:, :, k] along its second dimension:\n\nB = fft(A, 2)\nslices = [fft(A[:, :, k], 2) for k in 1:size(A, 3)]\nall(B[:, :, k] ≈ slices[k] for k in 1:size(A, 3))\ntrue\n\nRegions can be several dimensions. The transform is separable, so transforming along 1 and then 3 equals transforming along both at once:\n\nfft(A, (1, 3)) ≈ fft(fft(A, 1), 3)\ntrue","title":"Transforms along dimensions"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/dimensions/#Plans-for-a-region","page":"Transforms along dimensions","text":"The plan is made for one size and one region; plan_fft(A, (1, 3)) is the plan for that combination. Dimensions that share the length and the stride class share a kernel, so a plan for a 3D array costs little more than one for a 1D line.","title":"Plans for a region"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/dimensions/#Passes:-one-dimension-of-a-strided-array","page":"Transforms along dimensions","text":"FFTL.plan_pass transforms along one dimension of a strided view, applied to all lines at once. It is the building block of the transforms above, and the entry point for libraries that build special variants of the transform themselves, such as NFFT.jl on its oversampled grid. Two optional declarations let it skip work:\n\nnonzero says that each line is zero outside the given ranges of indices along d. The first stage then does not read the zeros.\nneeded says that only the given output indices are used. The last stage computes only those.\n\nThe result on the declared outputs is identical to the full transform. Here, the input is zero outside its first 16 of 64 entries, and only the first 8 outputs are needed:\n\nn = 64\nV = zeros(ComplexF64, n, 10)\nV[1:16, :] .= randn(ComplexF64, 16, 10)\n\nfull = fft(V, 1)\npass = FFTL.plan_pass(V, 1; nonzero = (1:16,), needed = (1:8,))\nW = similar(V)\nmul!(W, pass, V)\nmaximum(abs, W[1:8, :] - full[1:8, :])\n0.0\n\nPasses are single-threaded by default. A caller that already threads over many arrays keeps threads = 1; see Threads.","title":"Passes: one dimension of a strided array"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/dimensions/#Passes-for-real-to-real-kinds","page":"Transforms along dimensions","text":"FFTL.plan_r2r_pass(v, d, kind) is the same for one real-to-real kind along one dimension (see Cosine, sine and Hartley transforms).","title":"Passes for real-to-real kinds"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/platforms/#Platforms-and-limits","page":"Platforms and limits","text":"Which transform works on which number type and platform. ✓ means implemented and covered by the test suite; ◐ means implemented but not tuned or not tested on that platform; – means not available.","title":"Platforms and limits"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/platforms/#Transforms","page":"Platforms and limits","text":"transform CPU, Float32/Float64 CPU, other AbstractFloats (BigFloat, Double64, Float128) GPU (KernelAbstractions) libfftl\ncomplex DFT (fft, bfft, ifft) ✓ threads, SIMD ✓ one thread ✓ tuned on Metal, measured on CUDA ✓\nreal input rfft, irfft, brfft ✓ ✓ ✓ at about complex speed ✓ r2c, c2r, also in place\nDCT-II, the orthonormal dct/idct ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓\nDCT-I, DCT-III, DCT-IV ✓ ✓ ◐ partly tuned ✓\nDST-II ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓\nDST-I, DST-III, DST-IV ✓ ✓ ◐ partly tuned ✓\nDHT, R2HC, HC2R ✓ ✓ ◐ not tuned ✓\nbatched 1D passes (plan_pass, plan_r2r_pass) with pruning ✓ – – –\nany rank and any subset of dimensions ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓\nany length (Bluestein above prime factor 31) ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓","title":"Transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/platforms/#Platforms","page":"Platforms and limits","text":"platform status\nApple M1 Max (arm64, macOS) development machine; codelets use the FCADD/FCMLA complex instructions; GPU via Metal\nAMD EPYC 7763 (Zen 3, x86-64 with AVX2, Linux) x86-64 development machine\nAMD EPYC 7702 (Zen 2, x86-64 with AVX2, Linux) x86-64 machine of the benchmarks\nLinux and macOS in CI quick tests on every push to main and on pull requests; full tests weekly\nNVIDIA RTX A6000 GPU via CUDA, measured against cuFFT\nAMD, Intel GPUs not tested\nWindows not tested","title":"Platforms"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/platforms/#Julia-versions","page":"Platforms and limits","text":" version\nthe package Julia 1.10 and newer (weekly CI on 1.10)\nthe libfftl build Julia 1.12 and newer with juliac","title":"Julia versions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/platforms/#Limits","page":"Platforms and limits","text":"quantity limit\nelement types, complex ComplexF32, ComplexF64; any Complex{T} with T a floating-point type on the generic path\nelement types, real Float32, Float64, and every other AbstractFloat on the generic path; Float16 and integers are converted\nrank any\nlength of a dimension any; memory is the limit\nthreads Threads.nthreads() by default; a plan may use fewer\ntransforms on the GPU out of place; a plan holds about two buffers of the array's size\nlibfftl precision float and double\nlibfftl threads fixed at build time\n\nNumbers that are not in this table, such as the largest transform that fits on a particular machine, depend on the machine and are reported with the benchmarks.","title":"Limits"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/threads/#Threads","page":"Threads","text":"Plans take a threads keyword, which defaults to Threads.nthreads(). Start Julia with the threads you want (julia -t 8 or julia -t auto); FFTL does not start threads itself.\n\nusing FFTL, LinearAlgebra, Random\nRandom.seed!(6)\n\nx = randn(ComplexF64, 512, 512)\np8 = plan_fft(x; threads = 8)\ny8 = p8 * x\ny8 ≈ fft(x)\ntrue\n\nThe threaded result agrees with the single-threaded one up to rounding. Long one-dimensional transforms use a different algorithm on several threads (four-step), so their last bits can differ.","title":"Threads"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/threads/#When-threads-are-used","page":"Threads","text":"Threads only help when there is enough work to split. FFTL decides per dimension, from the number of values the transform touches:\n\nA transform with fewer than 2^15 values in total runs on one thread, whatever threads says. Splitting it would cost more than it saves.\nA batch of lines along a dimension is split over the threads as blocks of lines, each block sized to fit the cache of one core.\nA single long one-dimensional transform (a smooth length of at least 2^13) uses the four-step algorithm when it has more than one thread: the line is written as an n_1  n_2 matrix, transformed in both directions with threads, and the twiddle factors in between.\nA long line whose length has a large prime factor (at least 2^14) runs Bluestein's algorithm on threads.\n\nA 1D transform on a machine where some cores are busy is held up by the slowest piece, so fewer threads than cores can be the faster choice there.","title":"When threads are used"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/threads/#Nested-threading","page":"Threads","text":"Code that already threads over its own work should not also ask FFTL for threads. The oversubscription is worse than the parallelism it adds:\n\nchunks = Iterators.partition(eachindex(images), cld(length(images), Threads.nthreads()))\n@sync for c in chunks\n  Threads.@spawn begin\n    p = plan_fft(images[first(c)]; threads = 1)\n    for i in c\n      mul!(out[i], p, images[i])\n    end\n  end\nend\n\nEach task makes its own plan with threads = 1; see Plans and reuse.","title":"Nested threading"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/threads/#Plans-are-not-shared-between-tasks","page":"Threads","text":"A plan owns its work buffers and its thread buffers. Two tasks applying one plan at the same time corrupt each other's results. Make one plan per task (see Plans and reuse).","title":"Plans are not shared between tasks"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/threads/#Apple-silicon","page":"Threads","text":"With -t auto, Julia starts one thread per core, efficiency cores included. A thread on an efficiency core can hold up a threaded pass, so on Apple silicon start Julia with the number of performance cores (julia -t 8 on an M1 Max). Measured on the M1 Max, the 64³ transform with 8 threads was slower when the efficiency cores took part. The libfftl build uses the performance cores for the same reason (see libfftl).","title":"Apple silicon"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/benchmarks/#Benchmarks","page":"Benchmarks","text":"warning: Interim numbers\nThe figures and numbers on this page are the development state: one round per case, measured on the machines listed at the bottom under their normal load, at the commits given below. The measurement campaign on quiet machines will replace them; the protocol below is already the one the campaign follows.\n\nThe numbers in the text are read from the summaries that the figure scripts write (bench/fig_speedup.jl, bench/fig_standing.jl), so text and figures come from the same data.","title":"Benchmarks"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/benchmarks/#Which-library-to-depend-on","page":"Benchmarks","text":"(Image: The standing of every library against its best counterpart on each platform)\n\nThis figure asks the question a package author faces: which single library to depend on. Per case, the fold change is the time of the fastest other library divided by this library's time; right of the parity line is a win, left of it a loss, and wins are not clipped at 1. The box is the quartile rectangle with the median line and Tukey whiskers, the points are the raw cases. Colour marks the library. The label under each column carries the fold factor (the geometric mean of the fold changes over the cases the library itself computes), the win rate, and the coverage: libraries that lack transform kinds (ducc0 without DCT/DST, vDSP with c2c and DCT-II only, MPSGraph and cuFFT without real-to-real at all) compute their factor over a smaller subset, so the coverage is part of the label.\n\nFFTL is the only library that runs in all six panels and computes every kind. Its fold factor ranges from 0.88× to 1.39× over the panels. It is below 1 on the NVIDIA A6000 (0.88×), where VkFFT has the highest factor (1.03×); see Platforms and limits.\n\n","title":"Which library to depend on"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/benchmarks/#How-FFTL-compares","page":"Benchmarks","text":"(Image: Speed-up of FFTL over the fastest other library on each platform)\n\nThe speed-up is the time of the fastest other library for that case divided by FFTL's time; above 1 (shaded), FFTL is faster. One marker per transform; colour is the precision, shape the kind; open triangles at the panel edge mark values beyond the axis. In the two multi-thread panels every library is timed at its better setting per case, the minimum over one and all threads, so FFTW is not charged for spreading tiny transforms over many threads. Transforms that no competitor computes (DCT and DST in cuFFT and MPSGraph, most real-to-real kinds) are not plotted. The table gives the share of cases in which FFTL is faster than the fastest other library, and the median speed-up; on the A6000, small transforms are bound by kernel launches.\n\nplatform competitors FFTL faster in median speed-up cases\nApple M1 Max, 1 thread FFTW, ducc0, vDSP 89 % 1.33× 386\nAMD EPYC 7702, 1 thread FFTW, ducc0 74 % 1.23× 386\nApple M1 Max, 8 threads FFTW, ducc0 87 % 1.35× 386\nAMD EPYC 7702, 16 threads FFTW, ducc0 69 % 1.20× 386\nApple M1 Max GPU MPSGraph, VkFFT 73 % 1.24× 119\nNVIDIA RTX A6000 cuFFT, VkFFT 22 % 0.88× 119\n\n\n\nThe data is the overview set in bench/results. Each data file has a .meta file with its provenance; the table lists the FFTL commit and the start of each run.\n\ndata FFTL commit measured\noverview-a6000-gpu-kinds.csv 49648b3 2026-11-03\noverview-m1-gpu-kinds-0ab2ed0.csv fec5b70 2026-11-06\noverview-m1-t1-kinds-0ab2ed0.csv 9e9bcc2 2026-11-06\noverview-m1-t8-kinds-0ab2ed0.csv fec5b70 2026-11-06\noverview-reco2-t1-kinds-f825bee.csv f825bee 2026-11-06\noverview-reco2-t16-kinds-f825bee.csv f825bee 2026-11-06\n\n","title":"How FFTL compares"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/benchmarks/#What-the-campaign-covers","page":"Benchmarks","text":"Complex, real and real-to-real transforms on the CPU, 1D to 3D, Float32 and Float64, against the competitors that support the same kind. Each kind has its own column; kinds without a competitor are shown separately.\nThreads. The same sizes with one thread and with the number of threads of the machine, with the noise band of each machine.\nGPU. Complex, real and real-to-real transforms against the vendor library (cuFFT on CUDA, MPSGraph and Metal.jl's FFT on Metal) and against VkFFT.\nlibfftl. The C library against the Julia package.\nPlanning. The cost of the first plan in a fresh process, separate from the execution time.\nAccuracy. Error against a high-precision reference over the same sizes.","title":"What the campaign covers"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/benchmarks/#How-it-is-measured","page":"Benchmarks","text":"The protocol is described on the Performance page: A/B runs on the same machine, medians of interleaved runs, A/A runs for the noise band, and sizes reported separately from the sizes used for tuning.","title":"How it is measured"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/benchmarks/#Machines","page":"Benchmarks","text":"machine role\nApple M1 Max development machine, CPU and Metal GPU\nAMD EPYC 7702 (Zen 2, 2 sockets, AVX2) x86-64 CPU of the figures above\nAMD EPYC 7763 (Zen 3, 2 sockets, AVX2) x86-64 development machine; hosts the A6000\nNVIDIA RTX A6000 CUDA GPU\n\nEach result names the library, its version, the build flags and the thread count. Competitors are built from their current releases with native CPU flags; FFTW is planned with MEASURE.","title":"Machines"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/benchmarks/#Status","page":"Benchmarks","text":"part status\nharness, protocol and figures prepared\ncampaign (quiet machines, several rounds, held-out sizes) planned","title":"Status"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/performance/#Performance","page":"Performance","text":"This page describes what the design does for speed and how speed is measured. The numbers themselves come with the benchmark release, not here.","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/performance/#What-is-fast,-and-where","page":"Performance","text":"Speed depends on four things, and each has its own part of the code:\n\nKernels. Codelets are straight-line, SIMD and specialised for the radix, the element type and the vector width (see Implementation).\nRadix sequence. The rule in src/factor.jl decides how many stages a length takes and how much each stage moves through the cache. It was derived from exhaustive searches, and the measured effect of a choice is in the comments of that file.\nMemory traffic. Stockham chains never transpose, batches of lines are blocked to fit the cache of one core, and two dimensions are fused when their plane fits in cache.\nThreads. Work is split only when the transform is large enough to pay for it, see Threads.\n\nPlans carry everything that does not change between transforms, so the cost of planning is paid once. The plan cache and the precompile workload remove the compilation cost from the first call.","title":"What is fast, and where"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/performance/#How-speed-is-measured","page":"Performance","text":"A claim about speed is a measurement made under a fixed protocol. The benchmark release will follow this one:\n\nA/B on the same machine. Two versions run interleaved, one process per variant, on the same inputs. A change is accepted only when its median is better outside the noise.\nNoise is measured. The same version is run against itself (A/A) on each machine and thread count. A difference inside that band is reported as a tie.\nCompetitors at their best. Each competitor is built and planned with its own recommended settings, and the exact library and version are named in the results.\nHeld-out sizes. Sizes used for tuning are never used for the reported comparison; the reported set is drawn before the run.\nAccuracy next to speed. Every size is also checked for accuracy against a high-precision reference, so a faster kernel cannot win by being less exact.\n\nThe Benchmarks page lists what is measured and on which machines.","title":"How speed is measured"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/performance/#What-a-plan-costs","page":"Performance","text":"Three costs are separate in every measurement: the first plan in a fresh process (which compiles), the plan once compiled, and the transform itself. Benchmarks report them separately, because in an application they happen at different times.","title":"What a plan costs"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/performance/#Where-the-design-gives-up-speed","page":"Performance","text":"Other number types take the generic path, which has no SIMD and no threads.\nDCT-I and DST-I are computed through a real DFT of about twice the length, and DCT-IV of odd length likewise. The extra length shows in the measurements.\nOn the GPU, the real-to-real kinds are not tuned yet.\nBluestein transforms a line through two transforms of length M  2n - 1. It is used only where the smooth codelets cannot cover the length.","title":"Where the design gives up speed"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/libfftl/#C-library-(libfftl)","page":"C library (libfftl)","text":"libfftl is FFTL compiled into a shared library that C, C++ or any language with a C interface can call. No transform is written in C: juliac --trim=safe compiles the Julia package, through the run-time plans of Compiling ahead of time, and a thin Julia file (libfftl/src/libfftl.jl) defines the C entry points. A program that links the library needs no Julia installation; the Julia runtime it needs is shipped next to it. The interface is described in the C API reference.\n\nnote: Experimental\nThe library is built and tested in CI on Linux and macOS. The C interface may still change.","title":"C library (libfftl)"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/libfftl/#Building","page":"C library (libfftl)","text":"The build needs Julia 1.12 or newer with juliac (JuliaC.jl), which the build script installs in its own environment:\n\ncd libfftl\n./build.sh\n\nThe result is in libfftl/build/lib: the library (14 MB on macOS arm64) and the Julia runtime next to it (10 MB: libjulia and the few libraries it loads, without LLVM and without BLAS). build.sh also compiles the C test test/test.c against the library into test/test; run it to check the build.\n\nThree environment variables change the build:\n\nvariable meaning\nFFTL_THREADS number of worker threads baked into the library, or auto for one per CPU of the machine that loads it. Default: auto on Linux; on macOS the number of performance cores of the build machine.\nFFTL_TRIM safe (default): trimmed image, the trim verifier must resolve every call. off: an untrimmed library, for diagnosing a failing trim build.\nJULIA_CPU_TARGET the CPU target of the compiled code. The script picks apple-m1 on macOS arm64, x86-64-v3 on x86-64 and generic elsewhere. One target is used because a library built for several targets hangs in a garbage collection triggered by a second thread.\n\nThe thread count is fixed when the library is built. The Julia runtime cannot grow its worker pool after it starts, so changing it needs a rebuild.","title":"Building"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/libfftl/#Using-it","page":"C library (libfftl)","text":"A complete program that transforms a 2D array and checks the result against the input after a forward and backward transform:\n\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include \"fftl.h\"\n\nint main(void) {\n  int64_t dims[2] = {256, 100};        /* column major: dims[0] varies fastest */\n  int32_t region[2] = {0, 1};          /* transform along both dimensions */\n  size_t n = 256 * 100;\n\n  fftl_plan fwd = fftl_plan_dft(2, dims, 2, region, FFTL_PRECISION_DOUBLE, -1, 4);\n  fftl_plan bwd = fftl_plan_dft(2, dims, 2, region, FFTL_PRECISION_DOUBLE, +1, 4);\n  if (fwd < 0 || bwd < 0) {\n    char msg[256];\n    fftl_last_error(msg, sizeof msg);\n    fprintf(stderr, \"plan: %s\\n\", msg);\n    return 1;\n  }\n\n  /* interleaved complex values (re, im) */\n  double *x = malloc(2 * n * sizeof *x), *X = malloc(2 * n * sizeof *X);\n  for (size_t i = 0; i < 2 * n; i++) x[i] = (double)(i % 7) - 3.0;\n\n  fftl_plan_execute(fwd, X, x);        /* out of place */\n  fftl_plan_execute(bwd, X, X);        /* in place */\n\n  /* the backward transform is unnormalised: divide by n */\n  double err = 0;\n  for (size_t i = 0; i < 2 * n; i++) {\n    double d = X[i] / (double)n - x[i];\n    if (d < 0) d = -d;\n    if (d > err) err = d;\n  }\n  printf(\"max round-trip error %.3g\\n\", err);\n\n  fftl_plan_destroy(fwd);\n  fftl_plan_destroy(bwd);\n  free(x); free(X);\n  return err < 1e-10 ? 0 : 1;\n}\n\nCompile and link it against the build:\n\ncc -O2 -Iinclude example.c -o example -Lbuild/lib -lfftl -Wl,-rpath,\"$(pwd)/build/lib\" -lm\n./example","title":"Using it"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/libfftl/#Rules-to-know","page":"C library (libfftl)","text":"Column major. Arrays are stored with the first index fastest, as in Julia. A C array double a[rows][cols] is row major: pass its dimensions reversed, or transpose the layout.\nComplex values are interleaved (real part, imaginary part), 16 bytes per value in double precision and 8 in single precision.\nNothing is normalised. The backward transform multiplies by the number of transformed elements.\nErrors are values. Constructors return a negative handle on error, executions return -1; fftl_last_error gives the reason. A destroyed or invalid handle is rejected by fftl_plan_execute and fftl_plan_destroy.\nOne plan, one thread at a time. Different plans can execute in parallel. Do not destroy a plan while it executes.\nReal transforms are separate plan constructors, fftl_plan_dft_r2c and fftl_plan_dft_c2r, and real-to-real transforms use fftl_plan_r2r with one kind per dimension. The complex array holds the half spectrum along the first transformed dimension: dim/2 + 1 values there, the other dimensions as in the real array. In place, the real array is padded along that dimension to 2 * (dim/2 + 1) values, so that it occupies exactly the memory of the half spectrum, as FFTW pads it; the values at positions dim and above are ignored by r2c and undefined after c2r.","title":"Rules to know"},{"category":"section","location":"","page":"Home","text":"(Image: FFTL.jl)","title":"Home"},{"category":"section","location":"#FFTL.jl","page":"Home","text":"Fastest Fourier Transform in Lübeck: a fast Fourier transform written in Julia. Complex, real and real-to-real transforms of any length and any number of dimensions, in Float32, Float64 and other floating-point types, on several threads with SIMD on the CPU, through KernelAbstractions on GPUs, and as a C library (libfftl) compiled from the same Julia code.\n\nwarning: Experimental\nFFTL.jl is under development and not registered yet. The interface can still change; the documented parts are the ones tested in CI.","title":"FFTL.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#How-FFTL-came-about","page":"Home","text":"FFTL was written by a coding agent under the guidance of a domain expert. Julia makes a library of this scope possible in one language: the compiler generates the codelets for the radix, element type and SIMD width in use, the same generator serves the GPU kernels, and juliac turns the package into a C library. A coding agent makes it possible to implement, test and measure the many details such a library needs. The library builds on decades of work on fast Fourier transforms, above all on FFTW; FFTW and FFTL describes that history.\n\nFFTL is meant to be developed further by the Julia community, as one FFT that the ecosystem shares. Contribute says where help is most useful.","title":"How FFTL came about"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-FFTL-covers","page":"Home","text":"Transforms, number types and platforms, in Julia.\n\nComplex, real-input and all eleven real-to-real kinds (DCT-I to DCT-IV, DST-I to DST-IV, Hartley, halfcomplex), along any set of dimensions of any rank.\nAny length: smooth lengths through generated codelets, lengths with large prime factors through Bluestein's algorithm, no padding to powers of two.\nBigFloat and other floating-point types in their own precision, not converted to Float64.\nGPUs through KernelAbstractions.jl, with the same functions.\nThreads where the transform is large enough, and the four-step algorithm for one long line (see Threads).\n\nA C library made from the Julia code. libfftl is the package compiled with juliac --trim. No transform is written in C, and a program that links the library needs no Julia installation. The run-time plans it is built from are public, so Julia programs that use them can be trimmed as well.\n\nEntry points for special variants. Passes transform one dimension of a strided array and can skip zero inputs and unneeded outputs. Libraries that assemble their own transforms from such passes, such as NFFT.jl, use them.\n\nThe interface of the ecosystem. FFTL exports FFTW.jl's names with FFTW.jl's conventions and scalings, so using FFTL can take the place of using FFTW, and it is a backend of AbstractFFTs.jl's generic functions (see Coming from FFTW.jl).","title":"What FFTL covers"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/JuliaMath/FFTL.jl\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"using FFTL, LinearAlgebra\n\nx = randn(ComplexF64, 1000, 1000)\ny = fft(x)                  # all dimensions, unnormalised\nx2 = ifft(y)                # normalised inverse\nx2 ≈ x\ntrue\n\np = plan_fft(x)             # a plan holds the work buffers and tables\nmul!(y, p, x)               # apply it, as often as you like\ny ≈ fft(x)\ntrue\n\nr = randn(1000, 1000)\nR = rfft(r)                 # half spectrum along dimension 1: 501 × 1000\nirfft(R, 1000) ≈ r\ntrue\n\nC = dct(r)                  # orthonormal DCT-II; idct inverts it\nidct(C) ≈ r\ntrue","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-go","page":"Home","text":"If you want to read\nrun your first transform Your first transform\nsee the library used on real problems Tutorials\nmove code from FFTW.jl Coming from FFTW.jl\nsolve a specific task: plans, real data, threads, GPUs, C How-to Guides\nunderstand the mathematics and the algorithms Explanation\nlook up a function, a kind, a limit Reference","title":"Where to go"},{"category":"section","location":"#Design-in-one-paragraph","page":"Home","text":"A plan is a Julia object that carries the radix sequence, the twiddle tables and the work buffers for one size and one set of dimensions. Radix sequences are chosen by a fixed rule, and the codelets they run are generated for the radix, element type and SIMD width actually used. Long transforms run on several threads, and lengths with large prime factors go through Bluestein's algorithm. The details are in Algorithms.","title":"Design in one paragraph"},{"category":"section","location":"#Package-status","page":"Home","text":" \nJulia 1.10 and newer (CI: 1.10 and current)\nLicense MIT\nInterface AbstractFFTs.jl backend; plans are AbstractFFTs.Plans with mul!, *, \\ and inv\nPlatforms Linux and macOS (CI); Apple M1 Max is the development machine\nGPU experimental, via KernelAbstractions.jl\nC library experimental, juliac --trim, see libfftl\n\nThe complete matrix of transforms, number types and platforms is in Platforms and limits.","title":"Package status"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/spectrum/#Spectrum-of-a-sampled-signal","page":"Spectrum of a sampled signal","text":"The most common use of a Fourier transform: which frequencies are in a measured signal, and how strong are they? Here a signal of 1 s sampled at 8192 Hz contains two tones and some noise. The record is exactly one second long, so each frequency bin is 1 Hz wide and the tones fall on bins without leakage.\n\nusing FFTL, CairoMakie, Random\nRandom.seed!(2)\n\nfs = 8192.0                        # sampling rate in Hz\nN = 8192                           # samples: 1 s\nt = (0:N-1) ./ fs\nx = 0.8 .* sin.(2π * 440 .* t) .+ 0.3 .* sin.(2π * 1234 .* t) .+ 0.05 .* randn(N);\n\nA real signal has a spectrum with Hermitian symmetry, so rfft returns only the bins from 0 Hz up to the Nyquist frequency f_s2: N  2 + 1 complex values.\n\nX = rfft(x);\nsize(X)\n(4097,)","title":"Spectrum of a sampled signal"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/spectrum/#Amplitudes","page":"Spectrum of a sampled signal","text":"For a sinusoid of amplitude A the bin at its frequency holds A N  2 in magnitude, so 2X_kN reads the amplitude back. The bin spacing is f_s  N:\n\nf = (0:N÷2) .* (fs / N);\namp = 2 .* abs.(X) ./ N;\n\nPick the strongest bins above a threshold and report them:\n\nidx = findall(>(0.1), amp);\nfor k in idx\n  println(\"f = \", round(f[k]; digits = 1), \" Hz, amplitude \", round(amp[k]; digits = 3))\nend\nf = 440.0 Hz, amplitude 0.799\nf = 1234.0 Hz, amplitude 0.3\n\nThe two tones come out at 440 Hz and 1234 Hz with amplitudes close to 0.8 and 0.3; the noise stays well below the threshold.\n\nThe spectrum itself, with the bins above the threshold and the threshold line:\n\nfig = Figure(size = (640, 380))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel = \"Frequency (Hz)\", ylabel = \"Amplitude\",\n          limits = (0, fs / 2, 0, 1))\nlines!(ax, f, amp)\nscatter!(ax, f[idx], amp[idx])\nhlines!(ax, 0.1; color = :gray, linestyle = :dash)\ntext!(ax, f[idx], amp[idx] .+ 0.06;\n      text = [string(Int(round(fk)), \" Hz\") for fk in f[idx]],\n      align = (:center, :bottom))\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Amplitudes"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/spectrum/#Long-recordings","page":"Spectrum of a sampled signal","text":"The transform of the whole record is one call. For a long recording, a plan for the block length is made once and applied to every block (see Plans and reuse).","title":"Long recordings"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/spectrum/#Inverse","page":"Spectrum of a sampled signal","text":"Applying the inverse real transform to the spectrum with the two tones removed gives the noise alone:\n\nY = copy(X);\nY[idx] .= 0;\nclean = irfft(Y, N);\nround(sqrt(sum(abs2, clean) / N); digits = 3)   # RMS, the noise was 0.05\n0.05\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Inverse"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/fftw/#FFTW-and-FFTL","page":"FFTW and FFTL","text":"FFTW, the \"Fastest Fourier Transform in the West\" by Matteo Frigo and Steven G. Johnson, is one of the most important pieces of numerical software in existence. For more than a quarter of a century it has been the FFT that scientific computing relies on, and the reference every new FFT is measured against, including this one. FFTL's name is a nod to it. This page describes what FFTW achieved and what FFTL takes from it.","title":"FFTW and FFTL"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/fftw/#A-short-history","page":"FFTW and FFTL","text":"The fast Fourier transform entered computing with the paper of Cooley and Tukey in 1965; the idea goes back to Gauss, as Heideman, Johnson and Burrus showed in 1984. For three decades afterwards, a fast FFT on a new machine meant hand-written code for that machine.\n\nFFTW changed this. Frigo and Johnson started it at MIT in the late 1990s with an idea that was new for numerical libraries: instead of one implementation tuned by hand, FFTW combines many small, automatically generated kernels and lets a planner choose, at run time and on the actual machine, the combination that is fastest there (\"FFTW: an adaptive software architecture for the FFT\", ICASSP 1998). The kernels, the codelets, come from genfft, a special-purpose compiler written in OCaml that derives straight-line code for small DFTs symbolically, simplifies it and schedules it for the register file (\"A fast Fourier transform compiler\", PLDI 1999). The PLDI paper received the conference's Most Influential Paper Award ten years later, and FFTW received the J. H. Wilkinson Prize for Numerical Software in 1999.\n\nFFTW 3, described in \"The design and implementation of FFTW3\" (Proceedings of the IEEE, 2005), added SIMD codelets, the real-to-real transforms with their eleven kinds, multithreading and the guru interface for arbitrary strides and batches. Along the way, Johnson and Frigo found a split-radix variant with fewer arithmetic operations than any power-of-two algorithm known since 1968 (\"A modified split-radix FFT with fewer arithmetic operations\", IEEE Transactions on Signal Processing, 2007).\n\nFFTW's interface became a standard of its own. MATLAB's fft functions are based on FFTW, and vendor libraries offer FFTW-compatible interfaces so that existing programs can switch to them. Julia shipped FFTW in its standard library until version 0.7; since then FFTW.jl provides it, and the conventions of FFTW.jl are the conventions of the Julia ecosystem.\n\nThat a library designed in the late 1990s is still the benchmark for every new FFT on every new processor is the clearest measure of what its two authors accomplished. It is an exceptional achievement, sustained over decades.","title":"A short history"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/fftw/#What-FFTL-takes-from-FFTW","page":"FFTW and FFTL","text":"FFTL is written from FFTW's papers and documentation; FFTW's source code was not used. From these, FFTL takes:\n\nGenerated codelets. FFTL's symbolic generator writes straight-line DFT code for small radices, the idea of genfft. FFTL lets Julia's compiler do this when a radix and element type are first used, instead of generating C code ahead of time (see Algorithms).\nThe real-to-real transforms. The eleven kinds, their names (REDFT10, RODFT00, …), their unnormalised definitions and the halfcomplex order are FFTW's, so that code and formulas written for FFTW carry over unchanged.\nThe interface. Signs, scalings and function names follow FFTW.jl, and libfftl's in-place real transforms use FFTW's padded layout.","title":"What FFTL takes from FFTW"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/fftw/#Where-FFTL-goes-another-way","page":"FFTW and FFTL","text":"FFTL has no run-time planner. The radix sequence of a length comes from a fixed rule found by offline searches on the development machines, and the sequence is part of the plan's type, so Julia compiles the whole transform for it. Plans are therefore reproducible and need no wisdom, at the price of compiling each new radix sequence once. Stockham chains take the place of the decompositions FFTW's planner chooses, multidimensional transforms run without transposition, and one code base serves CPUs, GPUs and the C library. On the measured machines FFTL is faster in many cases and FFTW in others (Benchmarks); FFTW is always measured at its best setting (MEASURE, built with native CPU flags).","title":"Where FFTL goes another way"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/fftw/#References","page":"FFTW and FFTL","text":"J. W. Cooley and J. W. Tukey, \"An algorithm for the machine calculation of complex Fourier series\", Mathematics of Computation 19 (1965), 297–301.\nM. T. Heideman, D. H. Johnson and C. S. Burrus, \"Gauss and the history of the fast Fourier transform\", IEEE ASSP Magazine 1(4) (1984), 14–21.\nM. Frigo and S. G. Johnson, \"FFTW: an adaptive software architecture for the FFT\", Proc. ICASSP 1998, vol. 3, 1381–1384.\nM. Frigo, \"A fast Fourier transform compiler\", Proc. ACM SIGPLAN PLDI 1999, 169–180.\nM. Frigo and S. G. Johnson, \"The design and implementation of FFTW3\", Proceedings of the IEEE 93(2) (2005), 216–231.\nS. G. Johnson and M. Frigo, \"A modified split-radix FFT with fewer arithmetic operations\", IEEE Transactions on Signal Processing 55(1) (2007), 111–119.\nFFTW website and manual: fftw.org.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/abstractffts/#AbstractFFTs","page":"AbstractFFTs","text":"FFTL is a backend of AbstractFFTs.jl. Which methods each plan type provides:\n\nmethod complex plans real plans (rfft, brfft, irfft) real-to-real plans\nAbstractFFTs.Plan supertype ✓ ✓ ✓\nsize, ndims ✓ ✓ ✓\nAbstractFFTs.fftdims ✓ ✓ ✓\nmul!(y, p, x) ✓ ✓ ✓\np * x ✓ ✓ ✓\nAbstractFFTs.plan_inv (inv(p), p \\ y) ✓ (ScaledPlan) ✓ ✓\n\nReal plans are RFFTLPlan (rfft) and BRFFTLPlan (brfft, irfft); the complex ones are FFTLPlan; real-to-real are R2RPlan.","title":"AbstractFFTs"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/abstractffts/#The-generic-functions","page":"AbstractFFTs","text":"FFTL's exported fft, plan_fft, … are its own functions, not AbstractFFTs's. For generic code, FFTL adds methods to AbstractFFTs's plan_fft, plan_bfft, plan_fft!, plan_bfft!, plan_rfft and plan_brfft for arrays of floating-point numbers; AbstractFFTs derives the transform functions, the normalised inverses and the promotion of real input from them. So AbstractFFTs.fft(x) runs on FFTL when no other backend is loaded.\n\nThese methods are less specific than FFTW.jl's, which take StridedArrays of Float32 and Float64 values. With FFTW.jl loaded, the generic functions therefore run on FFTW for those arrays and on FFTL for the others (other floating-point types, non-strided arrays). Code that wants FFTL calls FFTL's functions; see Coming from FFTW.jl.","title":"The generic functions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/abstractffts/#Inverses","page":"AbstractFFTs","text":"inv(p) of a forward complex plan is the normalised inverse, a ScaledPlan wrapping the backward plan. For a real-to-real plan, inv applies the partner kind and the normalisation of the kind; the factors are on Cosine, sine and Hartley transforms.","title":"Inverses"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/image_dct/#Compressing-an-image-with-the-DCT","page":"Compressing an image with the DCT","text":"The JPEG idea in a few lines. A smooth image has most of its energy in few low-frequency cosine coefficients. The two-dimensional DCT-II moves the image into that basis; keeping only the largest coefficients and transforming back gives a compressed image.\n\nThe image is generated here, so the example needs no files.\n\nusing FFTL, CairoMakie, LinearAlgebra\n\nn = 256\nu = range(0, 1; length = n)\nimg = [exp(-((x - 0.3)^2 + (y - 0.6)^2) / 0.02) + 0.6 * exp(-((x - 0.75)^2 + (y - 0.25)^2) / 0.01) +\n       0.2 * sin(3π * x) * cos(2π * y) + 0.05 * sin(40π * x) * cos(30π * y) for x in u, y in u];\nsize(img)\n(256, 256)","title":"Compressing an image with the DCT"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/image_dct/#The-transform","page":"Compressing an image with the DCT","text":"dct transforms both dimensions. It is orthonormal, so the energy is the same in both domains (Parseval):\n\nC = dct(img)\nnorm(C) ≈ norm(img)\ntrue","title":"The transform"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/image_dct/#Keep-the-largest-coefficients","page":"Compressing an image with the DCT","text":"Sort the coefficients by magnitude, keep the top keep fraction and set the rest to zero:\n\nfunction compress(C, keep)\n  m = round(Int, keep * length(C))\n  thresh = partialsort(vec(abs.(C)), m; rev = true)\n  Ck = map(c -> abs(c) >= thresh ? c : zero(c), C)\n  Ck, count(!iszero, Ck) / length(C)\nend\n\nfor keep in (0.2, 0.05, 0.01)\n  Ck, frac = compress(C, keep)\n  R = idct(Ck)\n  println(\"kept \", round(100 * frac; digits = 1), \" % of the coefficients, relative error \",\n          round(norm(R - img) / norm(img); sigdigits = 2))\nend\nkept 20.0 % of the coefficients, relative error 3.2e-6\nkept 5.0 % of the coefficients, relative error 0.00014\nkept 1.0 % of the coefficients, relative error 0.0017\n\nThe error grows as fewer coefficients are kept.\n\nThe reconstruction from 5 % of the coefficients next to the original, and the coefficient magnitudes on a log scale, which show where the energy sits:\n\nCk, frac = compress(C, 0.05)\nfig = Figure(size = (720, 270))\npanels = [(img, \"original\"), (idct(Ck), \"5 % of the coefficients\"),\n          (log10.(abs.(C) .+ 1e-16), \"log |DCT|\")]\nfor (i, (d, t)) in enumerate(panels)\n  ax = Axis(fig[1, i]; title = t)\n  hidedecorations!(ax)\n  heatmap!(ax, d; colormap = i <= 2 ? :viridis : :inferno)\nend\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Keep the largest coefficients"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/image_dct/#Inverse","page":"Compressing an image with the DCT","text":"idct inverts dct exactly, up to rounding:\n\nnorm(idct(C) - img) / norm(img) < 1e-12\ntrue\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Inverse"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/convolution/#Fast-convolution","page":"Fast convolution","text":"A filter that slides over a signal costs O(nm) operations for signal length n and filter length m. Through the convolution theorem, the convolution is a pointwise product of spectra: transform both, multiply, transform back. That costs O(N log N) with N  n + m - 1. The result is linear, not circular, so both inputs are zero-padded first.\n\nusing FFTL, CairoMakie, Random\nRandom.seed!(3)\n\nsignal = randn(1000)\nkernel = [exp(-(k - 50)^2 / 200) for k in 0:100]   # a smooth 101-tap filter\nkernel ./= sum(kernel);","title":"Fast convolution"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/convolution/#Direct-sum,-for-reference","page":"Fast convolution","text":"function direct_convolution(x, h)\n  y = zeros(length(x) + length(h) - 1)\n  for i in eachindex(x), j in eachindex(h)\n    y[i + j - 1] += x[i] * h[j]\n  end\n  y\nend\ndirect_convolution (generic function with 1 method)","title":"Direct sum, for reference"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/convolution/#Through-the-FFT","page":"Fast convolution","text":"The padded length is a power of two here. FFTL plans any length, so any N  n + m - 1 works; powers of two are simply a convenient choice.\n\nn = length(signal) + length(kernel) - 1\nN = nextpow(2, n)\nxpad = zeros(N); xpad[1:length(signal)] .= signal;\nhpad = zeros(N); hpad[1:length(kernel)] .= kernel;\n\nThe inputs are real, so rfft and irfft do this with half the work of complex transforms:\n\ny_fft = irfft(rfft(xpad) .* rfft(hpad), N)[1:n]\ny_dir = direct_convolution(signal, kernel)\nmaximum(abs, y_fft - y_dir)\n3.3306690738754696e-16\n\nThe difference is at the level of rounding.\n\nThe signal, the filter and the output; the FFT points sit on the direct curve:\n\nfig = Figure(size = (640, 520))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1]; ylabel = \"signal\")\nlines!(ax1, signal)\nax2 = Axis(fig[2, 1]; ylabel = \"filter\")\nlines!(ax2, kernel)\nax3 = Axis(fig[3, 1]; ylabel = \"output\")\nlines!(ax3, y_dir)\nscatter!(ax3, y_fft; markersize = 5)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Through the FFT"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/convolution/#Filtering-a-long-signal","page":"Fast convolution","text":"For a signal much longer than the filter, one transform of the whole padded signal wastes memory, and a stream has no end to pad to. Overlap-add cuts the signal into blocks of length B, convolves each block with the filter through transforms of length N = B + m - 1, and adds the overlapping tails. The plans for length N and the filter's spectrum are made once and reused for every block (see Plans and reuse).\n\nlong_signal = randn(20_000)\nB = 1024 - length(kernel) + 1                     # N = 1024\nN = B + length(kernel) - 1\npf = plan_rfft(zeros(N))\npb = plan_irfft(zeros(ComplexF64, N ÷ 2 + 1), N)\nH = pf * [kernel; zeros(N - length(kernel))]\n\ny_ola = zeros(length(long_signal) + length(kernel) - 1)\nblock = zeros(N)\nfor s in 1:B:length(long_signal)\n  e = min(s + B - 1, length(long_signal))\n  block .= 0\n  block[1:e-s+1] .= @view long_signal[s:e]\n  part = pb * ((pf * block) .* H)\n  m = min(N, length(y_ola) - s + 1)\n  y_ola[s:s+m-1] .+= @view part[1:m]\nend\nmaximum(abs, y_ola - direct_convolution(long_signal, kernel))\n5.551115123125783e-16\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Filtering a long signal"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/implementation/#Implementation","page":"Implementation","text":"The algorithms explain what is computed. This page explains how the code is organised and why, for someone who wants to change it.","title":"Implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/implementation/#Specialisation-by-type,-not-by-configuration","page":"Implementation","text":"A plan is a Julia value, and its type says what it is. A Stockham chain is Chain{T,Rs,σ,K}: the element type T, the radix sequence Rs as a tuple of integers, the sign σ and the kernel type K are type parameters. The compiler therefore sees the complete stage sequence when it compiles the transform, unrolls the loop over stages where it can, and emits machine code for exactly that sequence. The price is one compilation per radix sequence, which is what the plan cache and the precompile workload are for.\n\nThe codelets follow the same rule. @generated functions build the straight-line code for a given radix, element type and vector width when they are first called with those types.","title":"Specialisation by type, not by configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/implementation/#Codelets-on-SIMD-vectors","page":"Implementation","text":"The arithmetic of a codelet is written on complex SIMD vectors (src/complexvec.jl). A vector holds several complex values, and the same instruction stream runs on all of them. For a Complex{Float32} array on a 128-bit machine that is two values per register. Lanes of the multidimensional transforms are the vector elements, so the same codelet serves a single line and a batch of lines.\n\nThe scalar versions in src/ka.jl run the same generated codelets on plain Complex values. That is how the KernelAbstractions kernels use the codelet generator without a second implementation.","title":"Codelets on SIMD vectors"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/implementation/#Kernel-cache","page":"Implementation","text":"Kernels are cached by kind, element type, length, sign and radix sequence. The cache has 128 entries and drops the least recently used one when it is full. A Bluestein kernel is two entries, its chain and its tables. clear_cache! empties it. Within one plan, dimensions with the same length and stride class share a kernel object.","title":"Kernel cache"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/implementation/#Cache-sizes-and-blocks","page":"Implementation","text":"The work of a batched transform is split into blocks of lines so that the work buffers of one block stay in the cache of one core. The cache sizes are read when the package loads: sysctl on macOS for the performance cores, sysfs on Linux for CPU 0. Where the query fails, defaults for the two development machines stand in. FFTL.cpu_caches() reports the values, and FFTL.set_cpu_caches! overrides them for a session.\n\nThe thresholds that decide between one thread and several, between the lane and the plane forms, and between the four-step and the plain algorithm live in one place, FFTL.TUNING (src/tuning.jl). Changing one changes the plans made afterwards.","title":"Cache sizes and blocks"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/implementation/#Thread-tasks-and-buffers","page":"Implementation","text":"Threads come from Julia's task scheduler. A threaded pass hands chunks of its work to a fixed number of tasks, and each task owns its own buffers, allocated when the plan is made. Two buffers per task are the minimum: the Stockham stages ping-pong between them.\n\nThat buffer ownership is why a plan must not run on two tasks at once. The plan is a container for the buffers, not a stateless function.","title":"Thread tasks and buffers"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/implementation/#Two-forms-of-the-same-chain","page":"Implementation","text":"There are two representations of a Stockham chain:\n\nChain (src/plan.jl) has the radix sequence in its type. It is the fastest form, and what the normal API uses.\nRChain (src/rchain.jl) has the radix sequence as data, and calls codelets from a fixed set of radices and signs in a plain loop.\n\nThe second form exists for the C library. juliac --trim removes everything it cannot resolve at build time, and a chain whose type is only known at run time cannot be compiled that way. RChain keeps every call resolvable. The price is a little run-time dispatch per stage, which is why the Julia API uses Chain. The libfftl how-to describes what the library is built from.","title":"Two forms of the same chain"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/implementation/#Lengths-and-the-factorisation-rule","page":"Implementation","text":"src/factor.jl holds the rule that turns a length into radices. Its header comment records the measurements the rule was derived from, and the constants in it are not tuned at run time. A change to the rule needs a measurement on the machines it is meant for: the rule that is best on one architecture can be slower on another.","title":"Lengths and the factorisation rule"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/implementation/#Element-types","page":"Implementation","text":"The fast path is written for Float32 and Float64. Other floating-point types use GPlan (src/generic.jl): a recursive mixed-radix transform on plain arrays, with Bluestein's algorithm for large prime factors, twiddles computed in the type itself, no SIMD and no threads. It is much slower, and its purpose is accuracy and generality.","title":"Element types"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/implementation/#The-GPU-path","page":"Implementation","text":"ext/FFTLKernelAbstractionsExt.jl holds the KernelAbstractions extension. Its kernels compute one pass of a transform in workgroup memory. The pass sizes follow the workgroup memory the device offers; the rules were measured on an Apple M1 Max, and the CUDA path on an RTX A6000.","title":"The GPU path"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/number_types/#Number-types","page":"Number types","text":"Float32 and Float64, real or complex, take the fast path: SIMD codelets, threads, plans with cached kernels. Other floating-point types take a generic path. It is slower, single-threaded and has no SIMD, but it computes in the precision of the type.\n\nusing FFTL, LinearAlgebra\n\nx = big.(randn(ComplexF64, 24))\nX = fft(x)\neltype(X)\nComplex{BigFloat}\n\nmaximum(abs, ifft(X) - x) < big\"1e-60\"\ntrue\n\nThe generic path covers the complex transforms, the real transforms (rfft, irfft, brfft) and the real-to-real kinds, for any length and any number of dimensions. Tests check the round trip for BigFloat to a few units in the last place of the type.\n\ninput computed as\nFloat32, Float64, ComplexF32, ComplexF64 fast path, the output has the same precision\nBigFloat, Double64 (DoubleFloats.jl), Float128 (Quadmath.jl) and other AbstractFloats generic path, the output has the same precision\nFloat16 converted to Float64\nintegers, Rationals converted to Float64 (or ComplexF64)\n\nA Vector{Int} is therefore transformed as ComplexF64. To compute it in BigFloat, convert the input first: fft(big.(v)).","title":"Number types"}]
}
